\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 No-exception laws, once too harsh even for anti- \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  Republicans, gain traction across US\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Kirchgaessner in Washington \par \uc2 1,271 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2022\par 06:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Extreme anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups are now emboldened to fight for laws that ban ending pregnancies conceived in rape or incest\par \par \uc2 Republicans were largely dismissive when, in 2019, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://web.archive.org/web/20210707042948/https://studentsforlife.org/2019/05/22/read-letter-to-gop/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a small group of extreme anti-\b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  activists}}}\uc2  called on the party to reconsider its \u8220\'20\'1Cdecades-old\u8221\'20\'1D view that laws restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US ought to exempt victims of rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 It {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/top-house-republican-mccarthy-says-he-opposes-alabama-abortion-law/2019/05/16/22fae790-77f3-11e9-b3f5-5673edf2d127_story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 simply went too far}}}\uc2 , the party\u8217\'20\'19s House minority leader, Kevin McCarthy, argued at the time, to support absolute \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that did not offer protection to women and girls who had been raped or were the victims of incest.\par \par \uc2 But there has been a sea change in Republican thinking since then.\par \par \uc2 At least 11 US states \u8211\'20\'13 including Alabama, Oklahoma, Missouri, Arkansas and Texas \u8211\'20\'13 have passed legislation that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without any such exceptions. Where Republicans once believed that absolute bans were unpalatable and \u8220\'20\'1Ctoxic\u8221\'20\'1D with voters, the party\u8217\'20\'19s legislators have now adopted the language once promoted by the most extreme anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists in the country who say any such exceptions are \u8220\'20\'1Cprejudice against children conceived in rape and incest\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is our view that the value of human life is not determined by the circumstances of one\u8217\'20\'19s conceptions at birth,\u8221\'20\'1D said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://web.archive.org/web/20210707042948/https://studentsforlife.org/2019/05/22/read-letter-to-gop/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one letter by proponents of absolute bans}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CA child conceived in rape is still a child.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 While most of the laws have been blocked by US courts for now, the expected reversal of Roe v Wade, the landmark 1973 decision that made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal in the US, would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/04/us-supreme-court-abortion-explainer"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 almost immediately}}}\uc2 , or as soon as practical, put the bans into effect.\par \par \uc2 It means that in many states, restrictions on reproductive rights {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/04/14/extreme-abortion-laws/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 will be more extreme than they were before Roe}}}\uc2  was passed nearly 50 years ago.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSomething has changed, at least in Arkansas, and I perceive nationwide,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jim Hendren, a retiring state senator from Arkansas who left the Republican party after the January 6 insurrection and is now an Independent. While Hendren said he was proud of his long history as a \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-life\u8221\'20\'1D legislator, he said Arkansas had \u8211\'20\'13 until now \u8211\'20\'13 also always passed legislation with rape and incest exceptions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe fact is, it\u8217\'20\'19s a different ethical dilemma when you\u8217\'20\'19re talking about a 10-year-old girl who is a rape victim being responsible for the actions of a criminal, versus someone who is responsible for their own actions,\u8221\'20\'1D Hendren said.\par \par \uc2 After years of \u8220\'20\'1Cclosing every other loophole\u8221\'20\'1D, Hendren added, \u8220\'20\'1Csuddenly the discussion changed\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou get to a place where there is nothing else to do. And there is no other mechanism for these [anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] organizations that have survived and thrived by working on this issue than to continue to push into this new area,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Hendren, who noted that Arkansas had one of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/pressroom/sosmap/infant_mortality_rates/infant_mortality.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 worst infant mortality rates}}}\uc2  in the US, ultimately voted \u8220\'20\'1Cpresent\u8221\'20\'1D on the bill. While he drafted an amendment that would have included a rape and incest exception in the legislation, he did not put the amendment forward for a vote. It would not have had enough support, Hendren said, because even Republican legislators who quietly supported him and \u8220\'20\'1Cagonised\u8221\'20\'1D over their vote were fearful that supporting the amendment would mean they would be labeled \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-choice\u8221\'20\'1D by Republican primary challengers.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/ideas/archive/2021/11/abortion-law-exceptions-rape-and-incest/620812/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mary Ziegler}}}\uc2 , a professor at Florida State University College of Law who specialises in the history of reproductive rights, said it was difficult to judge what the exact impact of absolute \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans would be because so many sexual assaults and cases of incest are unreported. It was possible, she said, that the US could begin to see minors being forced to carry pregnancies to term, as has been the case in Ecuador and Argentina.\par \par \uc2 Republican \u8220\'20\'1Cindifference\u8221\'20\'1D to rape and incest exceptions was \u8220\'20\'1Csomething new\u8221\'20\'1D, Ziegler said, and could reflect how groups that support such absolute bans no longer feel they need to \u8220\'20\'1Ctread carefully\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 At the centre of the new movement is an advocacy group called Students for Life of America (SFLA), which has called itself one of the world\u8217\'20\'19s leading grassroots \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-life advocacy groups\u8221\'20\'1D. Among other legislative victories, SFLA has recently taken credit for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://studentsforlife.org/2022/02/02/why-every-state-needs-a-life-at-conception-bill/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 thwarting \u8220\'20\'1Cbackdoor efforts\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  to weaken the Arkansas legislation.\par \par \uc2 On its website, the group emphasises its work across college campuses and includes pictures of dozens of young-looking staff members who lead the group\u8217\'20\'19s outreach efforts. But tax records show SFLA is far from grassroots: it has become a financial powerhouse in the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement thanks to the support of wealthy donors.\par \par \uc2 Records show that the group transformed from an organisation with a budget of about $20,000 in 2004 to $12m in 2019.\par \par \uc2 It is co-chaired by \uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2 , the conservative lawyer who served for years at the helm of the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  and was the legal adviser to President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , where he had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/graphics/2019/investigations/conservative-courts-leonard-leo/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 extraordinary influence}}}\uc2  selecting the conservative judges \u8211\'20\'13 including Amy Coney Barrett and \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 who now serve as justices on the supreme court.\par \par \uc2 Leo is described in some tax records as SFLA\u8217\'20\'19s principal officer, which legally means he has the \u8220\'20\'1Cultimate responsibility for implementing the decisions of the organisation\u8217\'20\'19s governing body, or for supervising the management, administration, or operation of the organisation\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Richard Crum, who is listed as a \u8220\'20\'1Cfinancial services executive\u8221\'20\'1D on the group\u8217\'20\'19s website and \u8211\'20\'13 according to \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 appears to be a top executive at CapitalOne bank, is another co-chair.\par \par \uc2 Crum did not respond to a request for comment. Other board members include the former Wisconsin governor Scott Walker and Greg Mueller, a conservative strategist and president of Creative Response Concepts, whose clients have included Leo\u8217\'20\'19s Federalist Society.\par \par \uc2 While SFLA does not list its donors in its own tax records, tax filings by other groups who have donated to the organisation show that donors include: the Apollos Charitable Foundation, a Texas-based group run by the Hetland family, who previously owned a Houston-based insurer called American Financial & Automotive Services; the Prince Foundation, the Christian charity in Michigan headed by Elsa Prince, the mother of the Blackwater founder \uc2 Erik Prince\uc2  and his sister, former education secretary \uc2 Betsy DeVos\uc2 ; and Gerard Health Foundation, which was founded by Raymond Ruddy, a millionaire businessman and donor to conservative causes like abstinence-only education policies. Ruddy also serves on SFLA\u8217\'20\'19s board.\par \par \uc2 On its website, the group said it relied on an unnamed \u8220\'20\'1Cangel investor\u8221\'20\'1D for helping it to \u8220\'20\'1Cgrow rapidly\u8221\'20\'1D and become the voice of the \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-life generation\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The group\u8217\'20\'19s longtime executive director, Kristan Hawkins, did not respond to the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s questions about Leo or other board members\u8217\'20\'19 role in the organisation.\par \par \uc2 Hawkins, who has said that in her \u8220\'20\'1Cideal world\u8221\'20\'1D the pill and IUDs would be \u8220\'20\'1Cillegal\u8221\'20\'1D, recently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.studentsforlifeaction.org/victory-oklahomas-life-at-conception-act-supported-by-sflaction-signed-into-law-by-governor/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 praised the Oklahoma }}}\uc2  governor, \uc2 Kevin Stitt\uc2 , for signing a \u8220\'20\'1Clife at conception\u8221\'20\'1D bill which she said was \u8220\'20\'1Csponsored\u8221\'20\'1D by SFLA and would make all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  illegal, except in cases where a mother\u8217\'20\'19s life was in immediate danger. If it survives legal challenges \u8211\'20\'13 which it could if Roe is completely overturned \u8211\'20\'13 the absolute ban would take effect on 1 August and would make performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a felony, punishable by 10 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 A separate bill signed by Stitt this week makes all abortions illegal after six weeks and takes effect immediately. Oklahoma had become a destination for women in Texas seeking abortions who were prohibited from receiving the procedure following that state\u8217\'20\'19s near ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Additional reporting by Jessica Glenza\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220506ei56000rt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
