\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18A severe chilling effect\u8217\'20\'19:  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  bans will inhibit doctors\u8217\'20\'19 advice to patients, experts fear\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 1,173 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2022\par 06:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Extreme restrictions combined with tough-on-crime laws could put medical professionals in severe legal jeopardy\par \par \uc2 The end of a federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US would have profound and detrimental impacts on medical education, the freedom to practice medicine and patients\u8217\'20\'19 ability to seek medical advice without fear of prosecution, doctors and legal experts said.\par \par \uc2 The warnings come as a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/03/supreme-court-abortion-law-leak-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leaked supreme court draft opinion}}}\uc2  and accompanying reporting from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/05/02/supreme-court-abortion-draft-opinion-00029473"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Politico}}}\uc2  show a majority of justices voted to overturn Roe v Wade, a landmark decision that has protected the federal right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for nearly 50 years.\par \par \uc2 If the court\u8217\'20\'19s opinion does not change substantially from the leaked draft, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/03/what-states-abortion-clinics-access-trigger-laws"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 at least 26}}}\uc2  states would be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://theguardian.com/world/2022/may/03/what-states-abortion-clinics-access-trigger-laws"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 certain or likely to ban \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 . Until the court issues a final decision, expected in June, Roe prevents states hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from banning the procedure before a fetus can survive outside the womb.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf this decision ends up being similar to what [was leaked], this is going to substantially affect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, obstetrics care and healthcare more generally,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Nisha Verma, a Darney-Landy fellow with the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Such a decision would represent a seismic shift in the American healthcare landscape, with devastating consequences for medical care, education and the doctor-patient relationship.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt really starts with doctors, because overturning Roe will have a severe chilling effect,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lindsay Lewis, co-author of a report by the National \uc2 Association of Criminal Defense Lawyers\uc2  that studied the potential criminal effects of ending Roe, and a board member of the same organization.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions are now more likely to contain extremely narrow exemptions to save the lives of pregnant people, severe criminal penalties for providers and to lack exemptions for rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 That puts doctors in the position of trying to interpret legislation that is often extremely narrow. In one recent example, an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/apr/12/oklahoma-governor-signs-bill-nearly-all-abortion-illegal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Oklahoma \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban}}}\uc2  makes performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a felony punishable by up to 10 years in prison. In addition, medical exemptions for the procedure are exceedingly narrow.\par \par \uc2 An \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be legally performed only in the event the medical emergency \u8220\'20\'1Ccannot be remedied by the delivery of the child\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CMedical emergency\u8221\'20\'1D is strictly defined as when a threat to a person\u8217\'20\'19s life \u8220\'20\'1Cby a physical disorder, physical illness or physical injury including a life-endangering physical condition caused by or arising from the pregnancy itself\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Functionally, these restrictions on medical exemptions have made new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans much stricter than even some that existed before Roe was decided, when some states allowed women to seek clearance for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from hospitals boards on the basis of psychological crises.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFor basically the past decade, or a little over the past decade, these exceptions have simply become so narrow as to be functionally unusable,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Elizabeth Nash\uc2 , an expert on state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions at the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a reproductive rights research organization. \u8220\'20\'1CThere are very few people who meet these definitions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In another example, should Roe fall, doctors who perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  against {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2019/may/14/abortion-bill-alabama-passes-ban-six-weeks-us-no-exemptions-vote-latest"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 state law in Alabama}}}\uc2  could be face up to 99 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis has the potential to turn the doctor-patient relationship on its head,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lewis. Doctors may not feel comfortable advising women they could go to another state to seek abortions, while women may be uncomfortable being open about speaking about her considerations.\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s more, tough-on-crime laws passed in the nearly 50 years since Roe was decided mean some zealous prosecutors could charge doctors, and a host of other people, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/nov/29/us-abortion-supreme-court-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 aiding and abetting, conspiracy or accomplice crimes}}}\uc2  related to the end of a pregnancy.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans would also have a dramatic impact on medical education, in particular on residents in obstetrics and gynecology who are required to learn routine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care as part of training.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLargely because of the politicization of obstetric and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, the public sees these as two very different and separate things,\u8221\'20\'1D when in fact, \u8220\'20\'1Cpregnancy management and pregnancy termination are very interconnected,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Kavita Vinekar, assistant professor at the \uc2 University of California Los Angeles\uc2  school of medicine and a fellow with Physicians for Reproductive Health. \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of the skills we use in obstetrics are directly related to the training we receive in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://journals.lww.com/greenjournal/Fulltext/9900/Projected_Implications_of_Overturning_Roe_v_Wade.449.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study co-authored by Vinekar}}}\uc2  found that nearly half (44%) of obstetrics and gynecology residents, or about 2,600 residents, will attend programs in states that could outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  training.\par \par \uc2 Already, some programs have pioneered efforts to help residents from Texas, which banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at six weeks in 2021 September, to travel to states where it is legal. But it is not simple or easy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m trying to think of ways to help, so there are not huge training and education deficits in our workforce, but I am also very nervous about it,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Jody Steinauer, a co-author of the study and director of the Ryan residency and training program at the Bixby Center for Global Reproductive Health at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Additionally, Steinauer said she worries that workforce shortages will threaten pregnant people in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned, because few doctors will want to practice under the threat of prosecution, unable to provide safe, evidence-based care.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CJust the emotional distress factor would be enough to make people not want to practice in these states,\u8221\'20\'1D Steinauer said.\par \par \uc2 Existing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, even as Roe stands, means pregnant people may seek \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care far from where they live. Verma has seen patients come to Georgia from Alabama and Tennessee, which have more restrictions. However, the threat of prosecution may pose a threat.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn too many of these cases,\u8221\'20\'1D a person who was prosecuted \u8220\'20\'1Chas come to the attention of law enforcement by someone from whom they\u8217\'20\'19ve sought medical care,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jill Adams, executive director of If/When/How, a reproductive rights legal group.\par \par \uc2 Although anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawmakers often argue they exempt women from punitive statutes, laws that confer rights on fetuses have already resulted in prosecutions. Those criminal sanctions are likely to expand should Roe fall.\par \par \uc2 That was probably the case for Lizelle Herrera, a Texas woman who was charged with murder for a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/think/opinion/lizelle-herreras-texas-abortion-arrest-warning-rcna24639"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 self-induced \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. Charges were later dropped \u8211\'20\'13 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal because Roe remains the law of the land \u8211\'20\'13 but historians warn the case is a forewarning of things to come. There is no requirement for healthcare providers to report pregnant people to law enforcement for self-managed abortions, which are legal.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn a time when we\u8217\'20\'19re trying to end mass incarceration, we will see far more women, far more people of color, far more poor people \u8211\'20\'13 these are the people we see getting abortions \u8211\'20\'13 being charged with crimes and going to jail simply for exercising what once was their right to determine what to do with their own bodies when it comes to pregnancy,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lewis.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220506ei56000rv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
