\par\par \uc2 Law\par \b \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  justices on  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 what they\u8217\'20\'19ve said and how they\u8217\'20\'19ve voted\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Michael and agencies \par \uc2 1,260 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par 15:28 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Following publication of the opinion that would overturn Roe v Wade, the court\u8217\'20\'19s views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are under extreme scrutiny\par \par \uc2 With Roe v Wade on the brink of defeat, following the leak of a supreme court opinion written by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and signed by four other conservative judges, the court\u8217\'20\'19s views \u8211\'20\'13 and track record \u8211\'20\'13 on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are under extreme scrutiny. Here\u8217\'20\'19s what the nine justices have actually said over the years about it.\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\par \par \uc2 Roberts voted for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in two major cases: in 2007, to uphold a ban on what opponents call \u8220\'20\'1Cpartial-birth \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D, and in dissent in 2016, when the court struck down Texas restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics. But when a virtually identical law from Louisiana came before the court in 2020, Roberts voted against it \u8211\'20\'13 and wrote the opinion striking it down, insisting the 2016 case \u8220\'20\'1Cwas wrongly decided\u8221\'20\'1D but that the question nevertheless was \u8220\'20\'1Cwhether to adhere to it\u8221\'20\'1D in deciding the 2020 case.\par \par \uc2 Roberts\u8217\'20\'19 views on when to break with court precedent could determine how far he is willing to go in the Mississippi case. At his 2005 confirmation hearing, he said overturning precedent \u8220\'20\'1Cis a jolt to the legal system\u8221\'20\'1D, which depends in part on stability and evenhandedness. Merely thinking that an earlier case was wrongly decided is not enough to overturn it, he said \u8211\'20\'13 it requires looking \u8220\'20\'1Cat these other factors, like settled expectations, like the legitimacy of the court, like whether a particular precedent is workable or not, whether a precedent has been eroded by subsequent developments\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Clarence Thomas\par \par \uc2 Thomas has repeatedly called for Roe to be overturned, and voted to do so in 1992 when he was a dissenter in Planned Parenthood v Casey.\par \par \uc2 In 2000, he wrote of Roe: \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a unique act, in which a woman\u8217\'20\'19s exercise of control over her own body ends, depending on one\u8217\'20\'19s view, human life or potential human life. Nothing in our federal constitution deprives the people of this country of the right to determine whether the consequences of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the fetus and to society outweigh the burden of an unwanted pregnancy on the mother. Although a state may permit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , nothing in the constitution dictates that a state must do so.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Stephen Breyer\par \par \uc2 Breyer, who announced his retriement earlier this year and will be replaced by \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  (whose own record \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thenation.com/article/society/texas-abortion-ketanji-brown-jackson/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 offers few clues}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D as to how she\u8217\'20\'19ll lean on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases), led the two supreme court majorities in defense of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, in 2000 and 2016, though he has acknowledged the controversy over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Millions of Americans believe \u8220\'20\'1Cthat an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is akin to causing the death of an innocent child\u8221\'20\'1D, he has said, while millions of others \u8220\'20\'1Cfear that a law that forbids \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would condemn many American women to lives that lack dignity\u8221\'20\'1D. Still, Breyer wrote, because the constitution guarantees \u8220\'20\'1Cfundamental individual liberty\u8221\'20\'1D, and because it has to govern even when there are strong divisions in the country, \u8220\'20\'1Cthis court, in the course of a generation, has determined and then redetermined that the constitution offers basic protection to the woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Samuel Alito\par \par \uc2 Alito, who wrote the majority opinion in the leaked draft, has a long track record of opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. He voted to uphold every \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law the supreme court has considered since his 2006 confirmation. Before, as a federal appeals court judge, he voted to uphold a series of Pennsylvania \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, including requiring a woman to notify her spouse before obtaining an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The supreme court ultimately struck down that notification rule in Casey and reaffirmed the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right in 1992 by a 5-4 vote.\par \par \uc2 In 1985, while working for the Reagan administration, Alito wrote that the government should say publicly in a pending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case \u8220\'20\'1Cthat we disagree with Roe v Wade\u8221\'20\'1D. He also noted he was \u8220\'20\'1Cparticularly proud\u8221\'20\'1D of his work arguing \u8220\'20\'1Cthat the constitution does not protect a right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\par \par \uc2 Sotomayor joined the court in 2009 with virtually no record on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues, but has voted repeatedly in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights since then. Recently, when the court allowed Texas\u8217\'20\'19s restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law to take effect, Sotomayor accused her colleagues of burying \u8220\'20\'1Ctheir heads in the sand\u8221\'20\'1D. Her displeasure with the court\u8217\'20\'19s recent Texas ruling was evident at a virtual appearance she made. \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t change Texas\u8217\'20\'19s law, but you can,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Elena Kagan\par \par \uc2 Kagan also has repeatedly voted in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in more than 11 years as a justice. She is also arguably the most consistent voice on the court arguing for the importance of adhering to precedents. She called Texas\u8217\'20\'19s new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law \u8220\'20\'1Cpatently unconstitutional\u8221\'20\'1D and a \u8220\'20\'1Cclear, and indeed undisputed, conflict with Roe and Casey\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Kagan had already grappled with the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before becoming a justice. While working in the Clinton White House she was the co-author of a memo that urged the president for political reasons to support a late-term \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban proposed by Republicans in Congress, so long as it contained an exception for the health of the woman. Ultimately, \uc2 George W Bush\uc2  signed a similar late-term \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban without a health exception. The supreme court upheld it.\par \par \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\par \par \uc2 Gorsuch has perhaps the shortest record on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  among the nine justices. He was in the majority allowing Texas\u8217\'20\'19s restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law to take effect. In dissent in 2020, he would have upheld Louisiana\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic restrictions. But Gorsuch insisted at his Senate confirmation hearing that he was concerned about procedural issues, not the subject matter. \u8220\'20\'1CI do not care if the case is about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or widgets or anything else,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\par \par \uc2 Trump named Kavanaugh to the supreme court shortly after he sided with the administration in a 2017 case involving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He later dissented from the Louisiana decision, and voted to allow the new Texas law to take effect, though he has taken a less absolutist stance than some of his conservative colleagues. In the Louisiana case, for example, Kavanaugh wrote that more information was needed about how the state\u8217\'20\'19s restrictions on clinics would affect doctors who provide abortions; and although he voted to allow the Texas law to go into effect, during oral arguments he appeared to have doubts about its novel structure and whether it would lead to a spate of copycat laws, both on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other rights protected by the constitution.\par \par \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\par \par \uc2 Barrett has a long record of personal opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, co-authoring a 1998 law review article that said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is \u8220\'20\'1Calways immoral\u8221\'20\'1D. Her one public vote on the supreme court concerning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was to allow the Texas \u8220\'20\'1Cfetal heartbeat\u8221\'20\'1D law to take effect, though she joined Kavanaugh in raising skeptical questions about its structure. She also cast two votes as an appeals court judge to reconsider rulings that blocked Indiana \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, before Trump\u8217\'20\'19s election victory, she spoke about how she thought \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law might change if Trump had the chance to appoint justices. \u8220\'20\'1CRoe\u8217\'20\'19s core holding that, you know, women have a right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 I don\u8217\'20\'19t think that would change,\u8221\'20\'1D said Barrett, then a Notre Dame law professor. She said limits on what she called \u8220\'20\'1Cvery late-term abortions\u8221\'20\'1D and restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics would be more likely to be upheld.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220504ei540028t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
