\par\par \uc2 Culture\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  on screen: how film and TV depictions have changed over time\b0\par\par\uc2 Adrian Horton \par \uc2 1,443 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par 14:46 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Screenwriters have been quietly raising the alarm over a shift in access to abortions in the US, showing both the difficulties and the history that\u8217\'20\'19s led us here\par \par \uc2 The supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/05/02/supreme-court-abortion-draft-opinion-00029473"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leaked draft opinion overturning Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 , the 1973 decision protecting a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US, is not official yet, but the ink might as well be dry. At best, Roe will be gutted to the point of near meaninglessness; it is very likely that come June, the ruling will be overturned entirely, allowing so-called \u8220\'20\'1Ctrigger laws\u8221\'20\'1D in 26 states {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/04/us-supreme-court-abortion-explainer"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to ban \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  as soon as possible. The US in 2022 will suddenly resemble the US in 1972, when a handful of states had legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and twomen sought out shadow networks of illegal providers \u8211\'20\'13 some dubious and dangerous, some not.\par \par \uc2 I don\u8217\'20\'19t think it\u8217\'20\'19s a coincidence that as a conservative majority solidified on the court, and as Roe\u8217\'20\'19s vulnerability became more clear, a handful of recent films have focused on the tense days just before legalization. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2022/apr/22/silence-guarantees-nothing-will-change-film-makers-challenge-the-anti-abortion-movement"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Happening}}}\uc2 , the French director Audrey Diwan\u8217\'20\'19s film based on Annie Ernaux\u8217\'20\'19s memoir, is a spare, haunting portrait of a young woman\u8217\'20\'19s search for an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1960s France. (The film, which premiered at Sundance in January, is out in the UK and arrives in US cinemas this weekend.) Phyllis Nagy\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2022/jan/24/call-jane-review-abortion-drama-is-sensitive-if-not-revelatory"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Call Jane}}}\uc2 , which also premiered at Sundance and will get a wide release in October, stars Elizabeth Banks as a late-60s suburban housewife who goes from patient to provider within the Jane Collective, a real underground \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  network in Chicago. The Janes, an \uc2 HBO\uc2  documentary recounting the history of the Jane Collective, will premiere in June, probably coinciding with the court\u8217\'20\'19s final decision.\par \par \uc2 Culture is not linear cause and effect, and it\u8217\'20\'19s impossible to say how film and television portrayals of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  play into this extreme regression. But they do offer an imperfect mirror to a culture in flux \u8211\'20\'13 one that, contrary to the court and state legislatures, has generally made slow progress toward depicting the reality of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which for many women is safe, uncomplicated and free of shame. It is a great irony that as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on screen has become less sensationalized and more realistic, the off-screen landscape for reproductive healthcare in the US has grown more hostile and misaligned with public opinion.\par \par \uc2 Take the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2021/jun/02/plan-b-unpregnant-movies-us-reproductive-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 burgeoning mini-genre of \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  road-trip movies}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 a handful of films from the last two years, disparate in tone but united on a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose, whose plots stem from the difficulties of obtaining reproductive healthcare in the US. Those films \u8211\'20\'13 Natalie Morales\u8217\'20\'19s Plan B, Rachel Lee Goldenberg\u8217\'20\'19s Unpregnant and Eliza Hittman\u8217\'20\'19s Never Rarely Sometimes Always \u8211\'20\'13 were in part a response to a wave of state-level crackdowns on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access passed in 2019. All three \u8211\'20\'13 two buddy comedies and a somber drama \u8211\'20\'13 took their teenage protagonists\u8217\'20\'19 decision to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or, in the case of Plan B, emergency contraception, as a given. Their drama hinges not on internal ambivalence but the real inane and cruel hurdles \u8211\'20\'13 age restrictions, hours of travel, money, so-called \u8220\'20\'1Ccrisis pregnancy centers\u8221\'20\'1D posing as medical care \u8211\'20\'13 to enacting her will.\par \par \uc2 In contrast to past films, which have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bitchmedia.org/post/films-and-tv-portray-abortion-as-more-dangerous-than-it-is"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 portrayed post-Roe \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  as more dangerous than it is}}}\uc2 , both the pre-Roe films and the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  road-trip movies depict the procedure as clinical, careful and undramatic. Call Jane includes a real-time procedure whose steps are repeated; Nagy\u8217\'20\'19s camera lingers over the metal instruments, the calm instructions of the providers, on several women\u8217\'20\'19s faces in anxious, determined repose, emphasizing the procedure as precise, professional medical care. Director Alex Thompson\u8217\'20\'19s 2019 comedy {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2020/jul/12/saint-frances-review-kelly-osullivan-taboo-busting-drama"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Saint Frances}}}\uc2  takes a similarly unsentimental approach to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , an increasingly common method for US women and one that barely reaches the screen. The procedure is unvarnished and straightforward: we witness Bridget (the film\u8217\'20\'19s writer, Kelly O\u8217\'20\'19Sullivan), a 34-year-old ending a surprise pregnancy, endure cramps and shed clotted blood over the course of an evening, then go about her life with all its other complications and contradictions.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s worth noting that the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement has their own arsenal of personal stories and cinematic depictions, often in lockstep with the religious right and the Republican party. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2019/mar/28/unplanned-review-anti-abortion-propaganda-is-a-gory-mess"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Unplanned}}}\uc2 , an anti-choice propaganda film, featured a gory portrayal of a 13-week-old fetus \u8220\'20\'1Ctwisting and fighting for its life\u8221\'20\'1D, as protagonist Abby says \u8211\'20\'13 a depiction numerous medical experts {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/08/movies/unplanned-movie-abortion.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deemed}}}\uc2  inaccurate and misleading. The film spread through word-of-mouth, faith-based groups and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thecut.com/2019/04/unplanned-anti-abortion-movie.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sold out theaters}}}\uc2  across the US, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.boxofficemojo.com/release/rl436831745/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 grossing $19m}}}\uc2  domestically. The 2021 drama {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2021/mar/24/roe-v-wade-anti-abortion-rightwing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 , a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theringer.com/movies/2018/7/17/17580316/roe-v-wade-movie-conservative-nick-loeb-cathy-allyn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 much-delayed and beleaguered production}}}\uc2  by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theringer.com/movies/2018/7/17/17580316/roe-v-wade-movie-conservative-nick-loeb-cathy-allyn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 siloed conservative Hollywood ecosystem}}}\uc2 , featured numerous rightwing celebrities including Jon Voight, Stacey Dash and Tomi Lahren.\par \par \uc2 On the television front, the rise of streaming services and peak TV helped proliferate portrayals of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as mundane and unapologetic \u8211\'20\'13 an aspect of a character\u8217\'20\'19s life rather than its defining trauma. Broadly speaking, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been one of the last frontiers for television, enough that the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 Kate Aurthur referred to it in 2004 as \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2004/07/18/arts/television-television-s-most-persistent-taboo.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 television\u8217\'20\'19s most persistent taboo}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. As Tanya Melendez {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vox.com/culture/22715333/tv-abortion-plot-storyline-lies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 outlined for Vox}}}\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on television could, until the mid-2000s with very few exceptions, be classified into three major plot lines: a character considers terminating a pregnancy but doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have to go through with it due to miscarriage or a false positive; the women\u8217\'20\'19s decision is resolved by a presumed instinct for motherhood; and a \u8220\'20\'1Cboth sides\u8221\'20\'1D plot which pits a woman\u8217\'20\'19s choice and those trying to stop her as equally understandable sides of a complicated moral debate.\par \par \uc2 There were a few exceptions \u8211\'20\'13 a 2010 plot line from Friday Night Lights, for example, is a rare example of an adult helping a teenager navigate parental consent restrictions in Texas. But things didn\u8217\'20\'19t change much until the 2010s, when Shonda Rhimes conquered network television and the rise of streamers fostered more opportunities for female creative talent and fewer content restrictions. On Rhimes\u8217\'20\'19s Grey\u8217\'20\'19s Anatomy and Scandal, main characters undergo \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 clean, clinical \u8211\'20\'13 without emotional distress or asking for permission. Streaming saw the rise, in the 2010s, of what Jezebel termed the \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jezebel.com/jane-the-virgins-xo-has-a-chill-abortion-1788241538"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 chill \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. On shows such as Claws, Glow, Sex Education, Shrill, Dear White People, Euphoria, Jane the Virgin and Girls, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a medical decision that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t derail the character, and is but one of many events in her life. On the other side of the coin in the Trump era, \uc2 Hulu\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s The Handmaid\u8217\'20\'19s Tale imagined a system of forced birth in the US \u8211\'20\'13 a dystopia that many awkwardly if not completely inaccurately likened to the Republican party\u8217\'20\'19s assault on reproductive rights in the 2010s.\par \par \uc2 Network TV appears to be catching up. A 2021 episode of NBC\u8217\'20\'19s hit This Is Us framed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a teenager as a traumatic event that required healing, but because of a toxic relationship, not the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself. The writer and executive producer KJ Steinberg {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ew.com/tv/this-is-us-producer-kate-abortion-choice-laurel-clues/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told Entertainment Weekly}}}\uc2  she wanted the flashback storyline, which features scenes from before and after the procedure, \u8220\'20\'1Cto reflect the seriousness of the decision without representing it as a life-defining trauma, because it\u8217\'20\'19s not. The trauma was the abusive relationship.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This is not to say that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on screen reached accuracy just as access crumbled. According to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ansirh.org/research/research/abortion-onscreen-2021"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ansirh.org/research/abortion/pop-culture?media_type%5B0%5D=49"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Onscreen}}}\uc2 , a project run by reproductive health researchers at the \uc2 University of California at San Francisco\uc2 , TV in 2021 continued to overrepresent white women receiving abortions (68% of portrayals; the majority of women in the US who get them are people of color) and underrepresent women who are already parents (14%, compared with 59% in real life.) Both TV and film have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/0959353516681245?journalCode=fapa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 underplayed}}}\uc2  the restrictions many women, particularly poor women and people of color, faced in obtaining an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  even with Roe on the books.\par \par \uc2 Film and TV have undoubtedly shaped our collective understanding of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 there\u8217\'20\'19s power in disclosure, in honest depiction, in busting taboos. But soft influence cannot, unfortunately, overturn a supreme court ruling. The latest mini-wave of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  films looking to the past \u8211\'20\'13 Call Jane, Happening, The Janes \u8211\'20\'13 could once be seen as a warning, now as a window to a regressive future. We shall see how the devastating shock of that will suffuse the next mutation of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on screen.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220504ei5400233\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
