\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18New peak in culture wars\u8217\'20\'19: global media react to Roe v Wade leak\b0\par\par\uc2 Amelia Hill \par \uc2 499 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par 11:55 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 US \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  row is dominating headlines, with international concern about \u8216\'20\'18terrible regression\u8217\'20\'19 for women\par \par \uc2 The prospect of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/03/biden-democrats-reaction-codifying-roe-v-wade-reform-scotus"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US supreme court overturning Roe v Wade}}}\uc2  has dominated front pages across the world, with the global media reporting in detail on one of the most polarising issues in American politics.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  leads on concerns that overthrowing the legislation represents a threat to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights for women everywhere. The newspaper quotes leaders from across the world in support of the 1973 ruling, including comments from the French senator Julien Bargeton that it would be \u8220\'20\'1Ca terrible regression for American women\u8221\'20\'1D and that \u8220\'20\'1Cprogressives around the world must mobilise\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Le Monde reports that the 98-page draft decision is \u8220\'20\'1Cvery striking in its unreserved and nuanced condemnation, never before formulated in these terms, of the entire legal architecture that has contributed to making \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a constitutional right\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Many papers carry reports on a growing divide in US society. Bild announces: \u8220\'20\'1CThe USA is heading for a new peak in the culture wars!\u8221\'20\'1D The judgment, it says, \u8220\'20\'1Ccould divide America\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 USA Today carries a similar warning, leading on Biden\u8217\'20\'19s assessment that if Roe v Wade is overturned, then a \u8220\'20\'1Cwhole range of rights could now be at risk\u8221\'20\'1D. El Pa\u237\'00\'EDs leads on Biden\u8217\'20\'19s comments that \u8220\'20\'1Ca woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose is fundamental \u8230\'20\'26 and the basic fairness and stability of our legal system demand that it not be overturned\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 El Mundo reports on the \u8220\'20\'1Crage of pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protesters [as it] explodes in front of the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , however, points out that despite the vow by Biden and other Democrats to respond to Roe\u8217\'20\'19s proposed demise, they are in reality powerless to stop the legislation being overturned, as the battle for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights shifts from Washington to the individual states.\par \par \uc2 Die Welt also focuses on the Democrats, this time in an opinion piece claiming they are also to blame for this \u8220\'20\'1Cdisaster\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CTheir mistakes are now taking revenge,\u8221\'20\'1D the piece states. \u8220\'20\'1CThe Democrats, with their political focus on identitarian issues and their sometimes openly displayed dislike of the rural population, are hardly present in many states.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Carrying photos of angry demonstrations outside the supreme court, as protesters brandished coat hangers that invoke images of back-alley abortions, the Wall Street Journal looks at the corporate reaction. Many, it says, have stayed silent, but a few have come out strongly in support of the protesters, including \uc2 Lyft\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the ride-hailing company that has a history of enacting policies in response to anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legalisation \u8211\'20\'13 Bill Gates and Sheryl Sandberg.\par \par \uc2 Corriere della Sera also approaches the story from a business angle, focusing on Amazon announcing that it will pay travel expenses for employees who need medical help, including abortions. This, the paper reports, extends the list of companies fighting against abolition in Texas, including Citi, \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Salesforce\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220504ei54001ml\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
