\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Contraception could come under fire next if Roe v Wade is overturned\b0\par\par\uc2 Melody Schreiber \par \uc2 685 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par 11:25 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 A reckoning with what the 14th amendment enshrines could affect consensual sex and even marriage rights\par \par \uc2 Following the bombshell release of a draft decision showing a majority of \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  justices may overturn Roe v Wade, legal experts believe other laws about individual autonomy may be in danger, including the right to access contraception.\par \par \uc2 Laws broadly banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may also prohibit certain forms of birth control that opponents incorrectly say are working as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -causing medications. And the supreme court decision underpinning the right to access contraception, Griswold v Connecticut, could also come under fire in much the same way as Roe did.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf this [draft] opinion becomes the opinion of the court, Griswold is imperiled \u8211\'20\'13 no question,\u8221\'20\'1D said Wendy Parmet, faculty co-director for the Center for Health Policy and Law at \uc2 Northeastern University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , in his draft decision, argues that Roe is a faulty law. Roe is based on the 14th amendment, as are decisions like Obergefell v Hodges on same-sex marriage, Loving v Virginia on interracial marriage, and Lawrence v Texas on consensual sex.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe opinion doesn\u8217\'20\'19t read like Roe was a wrongful tangent of the foundation\u8221\'20\'1D within the 14th Amendment, Parmet said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt really reads like the foundation was inappropriate and unfounded. So judges are going to find it hard to make the distinction with contraception.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 While Alito expressly states that similar laws are safe because they are not about \u8220\'20\'1Cmoral\u8221\'20\'1D issues as he says \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is, there\u8217\'20\'19s no reason the same logic couldn\u8217\'20\'19t be applied to other legal decisions, experts say.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf the draft becomes the real opinion, all of those issues \u8211\'20\'13 contraception, consensual sex and marriage rights \u8211\'20\'13 certainly are all at risk,\u8221\'20\'1D said Priscilla Smith, lecturer on law and reproductive justice at Yale Law School. \u8220\'20\'1CThey have definitely left the door wide open.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not explicit in the constitution, Alito argues in the draft. (That\u8217\'20\'19s partly because at the time of the framing, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was legal in the US before \u8220\'20\'1Cquickening,\u8221\'20\'1D when the movements of a fetus are first felt.) Similarly, contraception is not mentioned specifically in the constitution, so strict interpretations of rights could exclude it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are a lot of decisions that follow from the idea of a constitutional right to privacy,\u8221\'20\'1D Parmet said. \u8220\'20\'1COnce you throw down the best-known decision in that category of cases, every single other case is now up for grabs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The right to access contraception may not be challenged on its own, but it could be taken up under strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws \u8211\'20\'13 both new laws being introduced as well as older state laws on the books prior to Roe.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates have argued incorrectly that certain birth control methods, such as Plan B and certain intrauterine devices (IUDs), work as abortifacients because they may prevent the implantation of fertilized eggs. But there is no pregnancy without implantation, so these medications cannot terminate pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 Even so, it\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cdefinitely possible\u8221\'20\'1D that some states may criminally prosecute people who provide contraception that they claim are abortifacients, Smith said, \u8220\'20\'1Cbecause there are definitely states that would like to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the moment of conception, which means before fertilization, before pregnancy\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Some states could take these measures further to ban other forms of contraceptives.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s definitely going to be a huge push by the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  world to take this as far as they can,\u8221\'20\'1D Smith said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is a whole wing of this group that is opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  who only support sexual intercourse when it is within the context of marriage and when it is designed to procreate. The use of contraception gets in the way of that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Nationally, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates are \u8220\'20\'1Csetting the stage not just for a reversal of protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but also for protection for the right to fetal life \u8211\'20\'13 the fetus being a person with a right to life guaranteed under the constitution,\u8221\'20\'1D Smith said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s where we\u8217\'20\'19re going if things don\u8217\'20\'19t change.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220504ei54000rx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
