\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18It will be chaos\u8217\'20\'19: 26 states in US will ban  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  if supreme court ruling stands\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 983 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par 00:34 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 7\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Regulation would be returned to states where lawmakers in south and midwest have enacted bans in anticipation of court\u8217\'20\'19s decision\par \par \uc2 More than half of US states will outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  immediately or as soon as practicable, if a leaked draft decision from five supreme court justices remains substantially unchanged.\par \par \uc2 The result would send hundreds of thousands of people in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2021/10/26-states-are-certain-or-likely-ban-abortion-without-roe-heres-which-ones-and-why"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 26 states}}}\uc2  hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  elsewhere to terminate a pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 either by traveling hundreds of miles to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic or seeking to self-manage \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through medication from grassroots or illicit groups.\par \par \uc2 Many would also be forced to carry unwanted pregnancies to term.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is an essential part of reproductive healthcare, and this is going to affect people, even people who think, \u8216\'20\'18I will never have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Nisha Verma, a Darney-Landy fellow with the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Monday, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/03/supreme-court-abortion-law-leak-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 draft supreme court}}}\uc2  decision in arguably the most contentious case in generations was leaked. The case considered whether Mississippi could ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 15 weeks gestation.\par \par \uc2 The ban is highly significant because it strikes at the heart of US constitutional protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The landmark 1973 decision Roe v Wade established the right for pregnant people to terminate a pregnancy up to the point a fetus can survive outside the womb, roughly considered 24 weeks gestation, and a legal principle called \u8220\'20\'1Cviability\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The decision invalidated dozens of state bans, and until the court issues a final decision, prevents states from outlawing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before viability. A final decision is expected from the court in late June.\par \par \uc2 The leaked decision in the Mississippi case, called {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/dec/01/oral-arguments-mississippi-abortion-case-begin-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization}}}\uc2 , shows five conservative justices are willing to reverse constitutional protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the grounds Roe v Wade was wrongly decided.\par \par \uc2 If the decision is not substantially changed by the time a final opinion is issued, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulation would be returned to the states where lawmakers across the south and midwest of the US have enacted bans in anticipation of the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s six months to two years before the dust settles,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Elizabeth Nash\uc2 , interim associate director of state issues in the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a reproductive rights research organization. \u8220\'20\'1CIt will be chaos.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In that time, \u8220\'20\'1Cthere will be a lot of fluctuation as states are trying to implement their bans\u8221\'20\'1D, some of which are designed to go into effect immediately after a court decision is issued.\par \par \uc2 Such state bans would probably close \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics for nearly half of US women of reproductive age (41%) and increase the average driving distance to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider from 35 miles to 279, according to predictions from Professor Caitlin Knowles Mayers, an economist at \uc2 Middlebury College in Vermont\uc2  who has studied how the reversal of Roe would affect accessibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This would probably reduce the rate of abortions by 20% in states that ban the procedure and increase births by 4% (birth estimates are less certain).\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs was the case in the pre-Roe era, many women seeking abortions will find a way to get to the states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal,\u8221\'20\'1D said Myers. \u8220\'20\'1CCurrent empirical evidence suggests that about three-quarters of women in the states that go dark will manage to make such a trip, reaching providers in soon-to-be \u8220\'20\'1Cborder\u8221\'20\'1D states like Florida, Illinois, New Mexico and Virginia.\u8221\'20\'1D Myer also works as a consultant to the Center for Reproductive Rights and \uc2 Planned Parenthood Federation of America\uc2 , one of the nation\u8217\'20\'19s largest networks of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers.\par \par \uc2 Roughly 860,000 induced abortions are performed each year in the US. However, a disproportionate share of the people who seek abortions are low-income or people of color who already have children, making it more difficult to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCurrent evidence on the causal effects of travel distances indicates that about one-quarter of women seeking abortions will not be able to travel to obtain them and that most of these women end up giving birth as a result,\u8221\'20\'1D said Myers.\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s more, as women from states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal travel to states that allow the procedure, it could push up wait times for abortions in states where it is legal and perhaps send people seeking to terminate pregnancies even farther \u8211\'20\'13 like a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fivethirtyeight.com/features/it-can-already-take-weeks-to-get-an-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 secondary wave}}}\uc2  of medical refugees.\par \par \uc2 For example, California could see a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/04/blue-states-abortion-rights-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nearly 3,000% increase}}}\uc2  in the number of people whose nearest legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic is in the state. That could increase patient load from roughly 46,000 people a year to 1.4 million a year, practically overnight.\par \par \uc2 Already, most people who seek to terminate a pregnancy do so through {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/feb/24/medication-abortion-majority-2020-analysis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 medication abortions}}}\uc2 . Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a two-step protocol that safely terminates pregnancies up to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/womens-health-policy/fact-sheet/the-availability-and-use-of-medication-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 10 weeks gestation}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Activists have already {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/magazine/archive/2022/05/roe-v-wade-overturn-abortion-rights/629366/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 set up networks}}}\uc2  to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/apr/07/us-mail-order-abortions-oklahoma"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mail \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  pills}}}\uc2  to women in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal to self-manage an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . However, obtaining that medication would be illicit in states where the procedure is outlawed.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the immediate effects on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a decision giving control of the issue back to states would also substantially alter obstetrics care.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe find these laws are so extreme, clinicians are scared to provide evidence-based care,\u8221\'20\'1D said Verma. \u8220\'20\'1CThey don\u8217\'20\'19t know if they\u8217\'20\'19re going to be prosecuted or fined or criminalized.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Nearly half (44%) of all future obstetricians and gynecologists are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bixbycenter.ucsf.edu/news/almost-half-us-ob-gyn-residents-won%E2%80%99t-get-abortion-training-if-roe-v-wade-overturned"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 trained in states}}}\uc2  where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could become illegal, making it difficult to train young new doctors in care for early miscarriages, which use the same skills as induced \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or for ectopic pregnancies. The US already has among the highest rates of maternal mortality in high-income developed nations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis goes beyond induced \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D said Verma. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s going to affect people across the spectrum.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220503ei53002ea\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
