\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 US states could ban people from traveling for abortions, experts warn\b0\par\par\uc2 Melody Schreiber \par \uc2 989 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 May 2022\par 18:22 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 7\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 If supreme court weakens Roe v Wade, some states will take aim at people seeking procedures and medications out of state\par \par \uc2 As \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans proliferate in states around the US, some state legislatures are likely to go even further than just ending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their jurisdictions \u8211\'20\'13 taking aim at the growing numbers of people seeking procedures and medications out of state, experts warn.\par \par \uc2 If, as the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/02/roe-v-wade-abortion-supreme-court-draft-opinion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bombshell leak}}}\uc2  of its private vote suggests, the supreme court weakens or overturns Roe v Wade \u8211\'20\'13 the 1973 decision that established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 in an upcoming decision on Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, states will be left with a confusing patchwork of laws that will probably lead to legal challenges.\par \par \uc2 A fresh wave of restrictions will probably center around patients who leave their state to obtain legal abortions in other states, or who order medications to manage their abortions at home.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers in Missouri weighed legislation early this year that would allow individuals to sue anyone helping a patient cross state lines for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The law was ultimately {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://missouriindependent.com/2022/03/29/missouri-house-blocks-effort-to-limit-access-to-out-of-state-abortions/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blocked}}}\uc2  in the state\u8217\'20\'19s legislature, but experts expect such legislation to gain more support if Roe is weakened or overturned.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think states are not going to rest with just saying \u8216\'20\'18there won\u8217\'20\'19t be abortions in our state.\u8217\'20\'19 I think they\u8217\'20\'19re going to want to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for their citizens as much as they can, which would mean stopping them from traveling,\u8221\'20\'1D said David Cohen, professor at Drexel University\u8217\'20\'19s Kline School of Law and lead author of a forthcoming {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://scholarship.law.pitt.edu/fac_articles/517/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 article}}}\uc2  on cross-state legal issues that could arise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  context.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re going to see state-against-state battles that are really going to divide this country even deeper on this issue,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 If the supreme court overturns \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections, such travel bans might also be permitted to stand, Cohen said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe supreme court does not have well-developed case law regarding extraterritorial application of state law,\u8221\'20\'1D he added in an email. A court that has gone so far as to overturn Roe, he said, \u8220\'20\'1Cwould likely take that unclear precedent in the direction that is most anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But banning travel would go against \u8220\'20\'1Cbasic American principles\u8221\'20\'1D, he said. \u8220\'20\'1CYou have freedom of travel in this country, and as long as you\u8217\'20\'19re following the law in the state where you are, you are legally OK\u8221\'20\'1D under current law. For instance, adults can gamble in states where it\u8217\'20\'19s legal, even if they\u8217\'20\'19re from states where it\u8217\'20\'19s not allowed.\par \par \uc2 If the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is reversed, more than half of states are likely to prohibit abortions, according to separate analyses by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://reproductiverights.org/maps/what-if-roe-fell/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights}}}\uc2  and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2021/10/26-states-are-certain-or-likely-ban-abortion-without-roe-heres-which-ones-and-why"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Guttmacher Institute}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Several states have recently passed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that would be unconstitutional under Roe, but could stand if the landmark ruling is overturned by the supreme court. Some have passed laws similar to Texas\u8217\'20\'19s ban on abortions at six weeks of pregnancy \u8211\'20\'13 around four weeks after conception, when most people don\u8217\'20\'19t know they are pregnant \u8211\'20\'13 while others are advancing legislation similar to the 15-week ban at the center of the supreme court case.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Oklahoma lawmakers passed a Texas-style ban that will take effect immediately after Governor Kevin Stitt signs it, expected to occur within days. Stitt signed another bill earlier this year that would make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  illegal in nearly all circumstances, but that law would not take effect until August.\par \par \uc2 As state-based restrictions proliferate, traveling out of state for reproductive healthcare has become common. After the Texas law took effect last year, Planned Parenthood clinics in neighboring states saw an almost {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2022/02/24/politics/abortion-texas-clinics-surge/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 800%}}}\uc2  increase in patients.\par \par \uc2 If Roe is weakened or overturned, \u8220\'20\'1Ca lot of the states that are likely to lose access are surrounded by other states that are likely to lose access\u8221\'20\'1D, said Mikaela Smith, a research scientist at the Ohio State University\u8217\'20\'19s College of Public Health and the lead author of new {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lanam/article/PIIS2667-193X(22)00031-X/fulltext#%20"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 research}}}\uc2  on out-of-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  travel. That means patients may need to travel across several states to receive care.\par \par \uc2 That would also exacerbate existing inequities in healthcare, she said. \u8220\'20\'1CFolks who have the resources and have the financial means will be able to do the extra work to cross state lines and folks who don\u8217\'20\'19t, or don\u8217\'20\'19t have the connections or know how to access the care they need, just won\u8217\'20\'19t be able to.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 States are also likely to crack down on other efforts to access care. In Texas, a law {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/22/texas-abortion-inducing-drugs-law-greg-abbott"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed last year}}}\uc2  made it illegal to ship medication for self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including across state lines \u8211\'20\'13 another potential template for copycat legislation.\par \par \uc2 Since the \uc2 US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) \uc2 announced last year that it would continue its pandemic-era policy to allow medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also known as the \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill\u8221\'20\'1D, to be prescribed via telemedicine, the drugs have become a greater target from anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates. Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  now {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/feb/24/medication-abortion-majority-2020-analysis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 accounts for the majority of abortions}}}\uc2  in the US.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPills are going to be a major part of how people continue to get abortions after the supreme court rules, so I think that we\u8217\'20\'19ll see states trying to ban pills in all sorts of different ways,\u8221\'20\'1D Cohen said.\par \par \uc2 Blue states are preparing for the upcoming decision by shoring up reproductive rights for patients and protections for providers.\par \par \uc2 Soon after Idaho passed a contested Texas-style ban, neighboring Washington {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.forbes.com/sites/alisondurkee/2022/03/17/washington-enacts-law-blocking-texas-style-abortion-ban-as-more-states-copy-texas-law/?sh=6122f61c21b9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 enacted}}}\uc2  the first law to prevent lawsuits on performing or aiding an out-of-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Bills are progressing through the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2022/05/01/1095813226/connecticut-abortion-bill-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Connecticut}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.capitolnewsillinois.com/NEWS/illinois-house-democrats-pass-measure-protecting-providers-of-abortion-related-medical-services"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Illinois}}}\uc2  legislatures to protect patients traveling from out of state and the providers who care for them, and a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://calmatters.org/politics/2022/04/california-abortion-rights/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dozen bills}}}\uc2  are moving through the California legislature to make reproductive rights stronger and more accessible.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220503ei53001ba\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
