\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s coming  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ruling may spark a new era of US unrest\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephen Marche \par \uc2 1,140 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 April 2022\par 19:21 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s a strong risk that the case will spark anger and violence \u8211\'20\'13 whether the court overturns Roe v Wade or not\par \par \uc2 Civil wars don\u8217\'20\'19t always begin with gunfire. Sometimes civil wars begin with learned arguments. In April 1861, Confederate forces shot on Fort Sumter, but at the time even Jefferson Davis, the Confederate president, had doubts about whether the event mattered all that much. It was, he claimed, \u8220\'20\'1Ceither the beginning of a fearful war, or the end of a political contest\u8221\'20\'1D; he could not say which. During the decades that preceded the assault on Fort Sumter, complex legal and political fissures had been working their way through the United States, slowly rendering the country ungovernable and opening the path to mass violence.\par \par \uc2 The US is the middle of another such legal crackup, this time over the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The courts today face the crisis American courts faced in the 1850s: is there any way to make laws for a country with furious and widening differences in fundamental values?\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/apr/16/abortion-care-travel-us-reader-survey"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tell us: have you had to travel to another US state for an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 ? }}}\par \par \uc2 This summer, when the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  makes its long-expected decision in Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, it will inevitably alienate half the country. In anticipation of the overturning of Roe v Wade, several states have passed draconian anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, in the expectation that they won\u8217\'20\'19t be challenged. Idaho has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/15/idaho-abortion-ban-texas-law-six-weeks"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 already imitated}}}\uc2  the Texas law which allows private citizens to sue anyone who helps a woman procure an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a law that the supreme court has refused to overturn.\par \par \uc2 Two American blocs are emerging. In the south and parts of the west and midwest, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws are about to return to where they were in the 1950s. The rest of the country has already set itself in opposition to these laws. The division will not stay considerate and respectful, particularly in areas where liberal and conservative states neighbour one another. In anticipation of a post-Roe world and a flood of out-of-state patients, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/illinois-abortion-clinics-are-preparing-for-a-potential-post-roe-world-11638419374"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have established}}}\uc2  a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in Illinois, across the river from more conservative Missouri. Oregon recently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2022/04/06/1091036829/oregon-braces-for-abortion-ban-in-neighboring-idaho"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 invested}}}\uc2  in a $15m fund for medical refugees traveling from Idaho for abortions.\par \par \uc2 There are, right from the beginning, two reactions to the new division. The first is the use of force, as in the case of a 26-year-old Texan woman, Lizelle Herrera, who was recently arrested for murder for allegedly self-inducing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The local district attorney\u8217\'20\'19s office ultimately released her without charge, explaining that \u8220\'20\'1Cin reviewing applicable Texas law, it is clear that Ms Herrera cannot and should not be prosecuted for the allegation against her\u8221\'20\'1D. To be clear, current applicable Texas law doesn\u8217\'20\'19t apply to Herrera\u8217\'20\'19s case. When it does, they will charge people like her with murder. How far will the forces opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  take a custodial approach? Do they want to set up a DEA-style birth police? Any enforcement mechanism will also probably be highly ineffective. After billions of dollars spent on the war on drugs, the average price of a hit of heroin on the street is between $5 and $20. Women with means who want abortions are going to get them.\par \par \uc2 The second reaction to an America divided along \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lines will be interstate conflict. Missouri is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/03/19/travel-abortion-law-missouri-00018539"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leading the way}}}\uc2  here. A recent bill proposed a travel \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, explicitly focused on clinics in Illinois. This looks, on the face of it, like a straight violation of the 14th amendment, but the supreme court is a partisan institution and interpretation of the constitution now follows the partisan affiliation of the justices. They\u8217\'20\'19ll come up with something.\par \par \uc2 No matter what decision the supreme court makes, civil unrest will follow. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists will feel that their political system has failed them no matter what the court does. They have sacrificed everything \u8211\'20\'13 the dignity and integrity of their party, the value of their national institutions \u8211\'20\'13 in the name of getting enough justices on the court to enact this one legal change. If the court upholds Roe v Wade, they will quite naturally feel betrayed. If the court overturns Roe v Wade, they will discover a fact the new Texas law has inadvertently revealed: that the criminalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn\u8217\'20\'19t work. Their basic assumption, that the government can outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is simply untrue. At first, the Texas law appeared to cause abortions to decline by half. But quickly the numbers reasserted themselves. The decline is less that ten percent. Women went {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/03/06/upshot/texas-abortion-women-data.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 out of state}}}\uc2  or bought chemical abortions. The overturning of Roe v Wade will makes women\u8217\'20\'19s medical treatment more difficult and impersonal and humiliating. It won\u8217\'20\'19t change the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate significantly.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, from the other side, an overturning of Roe v Wade will be experienced as oppression pure and simple, especially given the number of justices appointed by presidents who did not receive the popular vote. In November 1860, five months before Fort Sumter, in the immediate aftermath of Lincoln\u8217\'20\'19s election, a judge in South Carolina announced that the state would no longer register indictments in federal court. Andrew Magrath, in a deliberate act of rejection, removed his judicial robe and folded it over his chair. He would now serve as a justice of his state, not his country. The audience recognized the gravity of the act. As one commentator at the time noted: \u8220\'20\'1CHere was a great political movement precipitated, not by bloody encounters in the street or upon the field, but by a deliberate and reasoned act in the most unexpected and conservatives of all places \u8211\'20\'13 the United State courtroom.\u8221\'20\'1D From that moment on, there were two legal systems. All that remained was the war. A similar breakdown in the legal system of the United States is already apparent.\par \par \uc2 Needless to say, this entire conflict is futile and stupid. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the United States is in rapid decline without the negligible effects of criminalization. The number of procedures dipped 19% between 2011 and 2017. If activists want fewer abortions, there are plenty of strategies that are vastly more effective than making them illegal. Canada, which has no federal laws of any kind on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has a fraction of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate of the US.\par \par \uc2 But that\u8217\'20\'19s not really the point. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is only a stand in for a fundamental conflict in political vision: morality against policy, community values against personal agency. There are two countries, at least, in the United States. The legal system is only catching up.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Stephen Marche is the author, most recently, of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.amazon.com/Next-Civil-War-Dispatches-American/dp/1982123214/ref=tmm_hrd_swatch_0?_encoding=UTF8&qid=&sr="}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Next Civil War: Dispatches from the American Future}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220427ei4r001bf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
