\par\par \uc2 Film\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Silence guarantees nothing will change\u8217\'20\'19: film-makers challenge the anti- \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  movement\b0\par\par\uc2 Rachel Pronger \par \uc2 1,227 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 April 2022\par 11:21 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Audrey Diwan\u8217\'20\'19s 1960s-set drama Happening is the latest in a wave of films on an issue that is increasingly topical\par \par \uc2 When Audrey Diwan first started writing a script about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , people would ask her why. Adapting Annie Ernaux\u8217\'20\'19s memoir about the author\u8217\'20\'19s struggle to obtain an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a student in 1960s France, Diwan knew the story was important, but it was difficult to persuade others of its relevance. Fast forward a few years, and no one is asking why. When {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2022/apr/20/happening-review-cleverly-structured-and-impeccably-produced-abortion-drama"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Happening}}}\uc2  premiered at the Venice film festival last year, critics were quick to draw connections between the plight of Anne (the character in the film) and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/apr/12/oklahoma-governor-signs-bill-nearly-all-abortion-illegal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tightening of \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  restrictions}}}\uc2  around the world. As it lands in UK cinemas this week, this period piece feels timelier than ever.\par \par \uc2 Happening arrives on our screens at a fraught moment. In the US, Republicans are continuing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/apr/08/oklahomas-move-to-ban-abortions-is-a-prelude-of-america-without-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a prolonged legislative assault on \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  as the supreme court waits to pass judgment on a case which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/dec/11/supreme-court-roe-v-wade-gay-rights-contraceptives-fertility-treatments"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 could overturn Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 . In Europe, the debate around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/apr/21/pills-in-the-post-how-covid-reopened-the-abortion-wars"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 regalvanised by the pandemic}}}\uc2 , and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-europe-55838210"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 last year Poland}}}\uc2  passed a near total ban, making it the sixth European country to impose severe restrictions. Elsewhere we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen a swing in the opposite direction, with moves towards decriminalisation in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2022/feb/22/colombia-legalises-abortion-in-move-celebrated-as-historic-victory-by-campaigners"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Colombia}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2020/dec/30/argentina-legalises-abortion-in-landmark-moment-for-womens-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Argentina}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2021/sep/09/mexico-ruling-decriminalise-abortion-activists"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mexico}}}\uc2 . The overall effect of this push-pull is an atmosphere of intense instability as we face up to a new phase in the struggle for reproductive justice.\par \par \uc2 This sense of history in the making has filtered down to film-makers. When Happening screened at Sundance this January, it appeared alongside two other period films exploring the subject. Phyllis Nagy\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2022/jan/24/call-jane-review-abortion-drama-is-sensitive-if-not-revelatory"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Call Jane}}}\uc2  follows a suburban housewife in 1960s Chicago called Joy who becomes a pro-choice activist after undergoing an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while Tia Lessin and Emma Pildes\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-27P9YQX9rc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Janes}}}\uc2  is a documentary about the real underground activists depicted in Nagy\u8217\'20\'19s film. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2022/feb/04/mahamat-saleh-haroun-abortion-drama-chad-achouackh-abakar-souleymane-lingui"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mahamat-Saleh Haroun}}}\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2021/jul/08/lingui-review-sacred-bonds-chadian-film-maker-mahamat-saleh-haroun"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Lingui, the Sacred Bonds}}}\uc2 , also follows a woman seeking an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , this time in contemporary Chad. Back in the US the recent emergence of the \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  road trip\u8221\'20\'1D sub-genre, sees films such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2020/may/07/never-rarely-sometimes-always-review-abortion-drama"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Eliza Hittman\u8217\'20\'19s Never Rarely Sometimes Always}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2020/sep/10/unpregnant-abortion-road-trip-hbo-max?share=tumblr"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rachel Lee Goldenberg\u8217\'20\'19s Unpregnant}}}\uc2  address the impact of restrictive legislation on women\u8217\'20\'19s lives.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not a new topic for cinema \u8211\'20\'13 the first Hollywood feature to tackle the subject {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2019/nov/30/streaming-stumfilm-danish-silent-film-archive-where-are-my-children-lois-weber-netflix"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was released in 1916}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 but the candid, explicitly political approach of this new wave of films feels revelatory. Historically Hollywood has either avoided the subject or relegated \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  storylines to moralistic subplots. There are some indie outliers \u8211\'20\'13 notably Alexander Payne\u8217\'20\'19s satirical Citizen Ruth and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2014/aug/31/obvious-child-review-gillian-robespierre-abortion-jenny-slate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Gillian Robespierre\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1C\b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  romcom\u8221\'20\'1D Obvious Child}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 but otherwise we\u8217\'20\'19ve had to turn to the European arthouse and films like Agn\u232\'00\'E8s Varda\u8217\'20\'19s One Sings, the Other Doesn\u8217\'20\'19t and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2008/jan/11/worldcinema.drama"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Cristian Mungiu\u8217\'20\'19s 4 Months, 3 Weeks and 2 Days}}}\uc2  for more nuanced, women-centred \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stories. Happening is part of this tradition; Call Jane, in contrast, is a much more unlikely proposition: a solidly middlebrow yet unapologetically pro-choice mainstream American movie.\par \par \uc2 While this new crop of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  films vary tonally, they are united by the immediacy of their message. Produced at a time of rapidly eroding rights, these films reflect the urgency of this context. Call Jane and Happening are both set in the past but share a fierce topicality. Although the film-makers offer different views of their period \u8211\'20\'13 Nagy takes the Mad Men route, with meticulous production design and dripping irony, while Diwan\u8217\'20\'19s vision is raw, immediate and spare \u8211\'20\'13 they both draw the same conclusion: this is a live issue. \u8220\'20\'1CEstablishing the story in the past tense could have given the feeling that the problem was solved,\u8221\'20\'1D Diwan tells me. \u8220\'20\'1CI wanted to point out the unfortunate permanence of this always contemporary problem.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 By re-creating a pre-decriminalisation era, Nagy and Diwan offer a clear warning of what we stand to lose. Films like Lingui, Never Rarely Sometimes Always and Unpregnant, however, remind us that for many the Bad Old Days are already here. For these directors, film-making is a form of activism. Haroun {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2022/feb/04/mahamat-saleh-haroun-abortion-drama-chad-achouackh-abakar-souleymane-lingui"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 briefly served as a politician in Chad}}}\uc2 , but quit because he felt making films was a more effective way to influence policy, while Hittman has described Never Rarely Sometimes Always as her attempt to speak directly to \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.polygon.com/2020/4/1/21181698/never-rarely-sometimes-always-eliza-hittman-abortion-stories-films-interview"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pig-headed, conservative men}}}\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D Unpregnant \u8211\'20\'13 a bawdy buddy movie about teens travelling from Missouri to Albuquerque for healthcare \u8211\'20\'13 couldn\u8217\'20\'19t be more different tonally from Haroun or Hittman\u8217\'20\'19s films, but Goldenberg has similar pragmatic purposes. Its use of comedy allows Unpregnant to connect with teens while also challenging stereotypes. \u8220\'20\'1CPutting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a pedestal where the only way to feature it is in a depressing well of sadness is very anti-choice thinking,\u8221\'20\'1D says Goldenberg. \u8220\'20\'1CThe comedy works as a critique; \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be this fucking hard to access! And the situation has gotten so much worse since we made the film, it sadly feels more relevant than ever.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 One of the ways in which these films challenge anti-choice ideology is by portraying the procedure itself. Both Call Jane and Unpregnant feature sequences in which we are calmly talked through the process step by step, and Nagy even follows Joy\u8217\'20\'19s procedure in real time. By contrast, Diwan graphically depicts the trauma of a secret \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  outside a medical setting. By keeping the camera running when most would cut away, these film-makers acknowledge a reality that has generally been hidden from view.\par \par \uc2 But perhaps these films\u8217\'20\'19 most important triumph is to humanise an issue that is often discussed in ideological terms. Both Goldenberg and Diwan tell me that they were drawn to these projects after having abortions themselves. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen I got an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  years ago, I told almost no one,\u8221\'20\'1D says Goldenberg. \u8220\'20\'1COnce I realised my own silence was adding to the stigma, I started talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to anyone and everyone \u8211\'20\'13 I wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t shut up about it!\u8221\'20\'1D Diwan similarly saw making Happening as an opportunity to force an unspoken reality into view. \u8220\'20\'1CShame invites everyone to silence,\u8221\'20\'1D says Diwan, \u8220\'20\'1Cand this silence guarantees that nothing will change.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 One film is unlikely to turn a hardened pro-lifer but breaking the silence on this issue at least provides an opportunity for dialogue. When I ask Diwan how audiences have reacted to Happening on the festival circuit, she tells me that she has been heartened by how willing people have been to engage with the film\u8217\'20\'19s politics. \u8220\'20\'1CDiscussion always took precedence over confrontation,\u8221\'20\'1D says Diwan. \u8220\'20\'1CMany men expressed their surprise and were able to say simply: \u8216\'20\'18I had no idea what a woman was going through at that time \u8230\'20\'26\u8217\'20\'19 I even debated with anti-abortionists stirred by the harshest scenes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 By challenging misinformation and upending stigma, films such as Happening, Call Jane and Unpregnant bring new perspectives to an age-old debate. As we continue to navigate a tense moment for reproductive rights, we need all the help we can get; cinema probably can\u8217\'20\'19t change the world, but perhaps it can at least change the conversation.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Happening is released on 22 April in UK cinemas.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220426ei4q002sj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
