\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Mail-order  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  pills become next US reproductive rights battleground\b0\par\par\uc2 Adrienne Matei \par \uc2 978 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 April 2022\par 12:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FDA\uc2  has dropped a requirement for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug mifepristone to be picked up in person \u8211\'20\'13 but some Republicans states are clamping down\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Oklahoma became the latest state to pass a bill to make performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a felony, punishable, in this case, by 10 years in prison and a $100,000 fine. The bill is expected to be signed into law by the governor, creating an even larger group of people \u8211\'20\'13 about 7.7 million between Texas and Oklahoma \u8211\'20\'13 who will have to leave their home state if they want an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Republican legislators are passing restrictions and bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in expectation of a supreme court decision in a crucial \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights case expected in June. Until then, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal, albeit severely restricted in some cases, across the US.\par \par \uc2 There was some hope that medication abortions might help alleviate this pressure. In July 2020, the \uc2 US Food and Drug Administration\uc2  announced that anyone seeking a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 that is, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by taking pills, up to 10 weeks into a pregnancy, without the need for an operation \u8211\'20\'13 would no longer have to pick up the medication in person, or take it in a doctor\u8217\'20\'19s presence.\par \par \uc2 Its decision to stop enforcing in-person requirements for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug mifepristone was a response to the Covid-19 pandemic, but in December, in consensus with the medical community, it ended the requirement for good.\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone, which data {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/features/2022-02-17/abortion-pill-mifepristone-is-safer-than-tylenol-and-almost-impossible-to-get"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  shows}}}\uc2  is safer than acetaminophen, is now available for US patients to acquire via telehealth services like \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Demand and Just The Pill, which offer online appointments and discreet mail order medications. Consultations and ordering are straightforward and user-friendly on these sites and pills are posted in less than five days. People may take it without a provider\u8217\'20\'19s oversight in the comfort and privacy of their own homes , just as they did after obtaining it in-person from a doctor.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decision empowered a wave of certified virtual clinics. In the coming months, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is expected to clarify how pharmacies, like \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  and CVS, can distribute mifepristone for the first time.\par \par \uc2 The increased accessibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication is a huge asset to people living in states that permit it \u8211\'20\'13 in fact, medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  accounted for 54% of all US abortions in 2020, up from 39% in 2017, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/feb/24/medication-abortion-majority-2020-analysis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to research}}}\uc2  from the pro-choice \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe were overjoyed [at the \uc2 FDA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s decision], because over the years we\u8217\'20\'19ve been building evidence that continues to show that people who want to use [mifepristone] to end their pregnancy \u8230\'20\'26 they are very capable of being able to read the instructions and do so on their own,\u8221\'20\'1D says Liza Fuentes, a senior research scientist at the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 However, 19 states currently have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/womens-health-policy/slide/state-restrictions-on-telehealth-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  laws}}}\uc2  requiring an in-person element to abortions, such as an ultrasound or an in-person counseling session, precluding a totally virtual experience. And some states, including Texas, Arizona, and Louisiana ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  telehealth services outright.\par \par \uc2 For those seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in states where it is restricted, acquiring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills through the mail is a much quicker and more affordable option than having to travel across state lines for an in-person appointment.\par \par \uc2 Already, says Rachel Rebouch\u233\'00\'E9, interim dean of \uc2 Temple University\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s law school, people seeking medication abortions can use social media to find advice on how to change their VPNs, have pills illicitly mailed to a \uc2 FedEx\uc2  drop-off point in a neighboring state, or how to have pills sent to someone else who can deliver them.\par \par \uc2 But Republicans are aggressively restricting access to telehealth care for abortions in red states, with many creating trigger laws aimed at banning or severely restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the board should Roe v Wade be overturned. And while some states have interstate agreements allowing healthcare providers to offer telehealth services across borders, providers are beholden to the laws of the patient\u8217\'20\'19s home state, preventing blue state doctors from mailing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication to red state patients.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are also attempting to amend anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills further to prevent red state residents from getting abortions regardless of where the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  takes place \u8211\'20\'13 even if they travel to a blue state to access in-person or telehealth care. Even if, for instance, a resident of an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  state traveled to California, these laws would see that their body remained under the jurisdiction of their home state. Some red states could likely already prosecute under {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/nov/29/us-abortion-supreme-court-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 existing accomplice and conspiracy laws}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou see states like Missouri trying to attach its laws to providers who are providing abortions outside of Missouri, but trying to think about how to civilly penalize those providers by saying \u8216\'20\'18any provider who provides an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a Missouri resident is bound by the laws of Missouri,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D says Rebouch\u233\'00\'E9.\par \par \uc2 Experts anticipate the brunt of aggressive anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  enforcement will be disproportionately borne by those with few resources \u8211\'20\'13 people of color, low-income families, and the young.\par \par \uc2 Blue states are working to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights within and beyond their borders, with some refusing to accept that state legislatures can impinge on the sovereignty of other states by enforcing laws beyond their own jurisdictions. California and Connecticut are both actively advancing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, working to shield \u8220\'20\'1Cproviders from legal liability and prevent them from being extradited\u8221\'20\'1D and by creating networks for travel for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and centers for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, says Rebouch\u233\'00\'E9.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe inevitable reality is that even though there is legal liability at stake, mailing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication and administering those pills at home can be really hard to detect,\u8221\'20\'1D she adds. \u8220\'20\'1CSome states will try to enforce [\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans] but others will not because it won\u8217\'20\'19t be worth the cost and it\u8217\'20\'19s going to be difficult to do.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220407ei47001jm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
