\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Are women\u8217\'20\'19s bodies private property? In Idaho, apparently they are\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 1,039 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 March 2022\par 13:06 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Brad Little\uc2  signed SB1309, banning all abortions after six weeks\u8217\'20\'19 gestational age \u8211\'20\'13 and it is enforced not through state action, but private lawsuits\par \par \uc2 If there was any doubt that Roe v Wade has been nullified, Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s governor, \uc2 Brad Little\uc2 , put an end to it on Wednesday, when he signed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://legislature.idaho.gov/wp-content/uploads/sessioninfo/2022/legislation/S1309.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 SB1309}}}\uc2 , known officially as the Fetal Heartbeat Preborn Child Protection Act, into law. Like Texas\u8217\'20\'19 SB8, Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s new law bans all abortions after six weeks\u8217\'20\'19 gestational age, in plain violation of Roe. But like SB8, the law may well be able to go into effect as scheduled next month, because it is enforced not through state action, but through private lawsuits.\par \par \uc2 The supreme court is likely to overturn Roe this summer anyway, in the widely anticipated Dobbs v Jackson ruling. But almost all abortions have been illegal in Texas since September, when the court gave its blessing to SB8\u8217\'20\'19s private enforcement provision and allowed the law to go into effect. Since the court gave the nod to SB8, other Republican-controlled states have rushed to pass copycat bills, eager to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  within their borders even before the downfall of Roe in a few months. According to the reproductive rights research group the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , SB8 copycat bills have been introduced in 12 states, including Alabama, Louisiana, Missouri, Ohio, Oklahoma and Tennessee. But Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s is the first to be signed into law.\par \par \uc2 The substantive impact of Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s SB1309 will be much the same as that of SB8: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics will close under threat of liability, and women in the state will be subjected to the material harm and moral indignity of not being able to make decisions about their own bodies, or personal decisions about the course of their own lives. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers will leave, or change their practice; clinics in neighboring, Democratic-controlled states, like Washington and Oregon, will see an influx of patients, reproductive refugees seeking in a different territory the treatment they\u8217\'20\'19re denied in their own. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nwaafund.org/info/idaho/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Local \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  funds}}}\uc2  will drain their coffers helping patients buy tickets for buses and planes out of state \u8211\'20\'13 that is, unless {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2022/02/23/texas-abortion-sb8-lawsuits/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 they get sued out}}}\uc2  of existence, too.\par \par \uc2 But SB1309 also differs from SB8 in several crucial ways, and those differences may highlight how the anti-choice right may govern in the post-Roe future. Unlike SB8, SB1309 allows an exemption for pregnancies caused by rape or incest \u8211\'20\'13 so long as those assaults were reported, that is, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.rainn.org/statistics/criminal-justice-system"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 which fewer than one-third of such incidents are}}}\uc2 , according to Rainn. The Idaho law has a bigger minimum reward for plaintiffs than the Texas law does: the private citizens who sue \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Texas will collect at least $10,000 if they win, but in Idaho, the prize is $20,000. And crucially, the Idaho law limits the range of potential plaintiffs. In Texas, any citizen \u8211\'20\'13 including those residing out of state \u8211\'20\'13 can sue over any \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  performed in Texas after six weeks. But in Idaho, in order to sue over an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a plaintiff needs to be a family member of the woman who had the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the man who fathered her pregnancy, or a family member of that man. Reported rapists are excluded from the right to sue, but their family members are not.\par \par \uc2 To pro-choice Americans, this smaller pool of potential litigants in the Idaho law might sound like good news. Fewer people with a right to sue over abortions means that patients and their medical providers have fewer enemies to worry about, fewer nefarious actors to look over their shoulders for. In Texas, denying women the right to choose has become everyone\u8217\'20\'19s prerogative, subsuming the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  into a state of generalized surveillance and vigilantism. Idaho isn\u8217\'20\'19t like that. In Idaho, denying women the right to choose is a private, family matter.\par \par \uc2 But the keep-it-in-the-family approach of Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s SB1309 has its own sinister and insidious logic. By granting a patient\u8217\'20\'19s parents, siblings, partners and in-laws the right to sue over her choices regarding her own body and life, the law implies that these parties have a particular interest in, or perhaps even ownership over, that woman. The logic is that women are the possessions of their families, and that those families \u8211\'20\'13 namely, their fathers and husbands, though the Idaho law extends to female relatives as well \u8211\'20\'13 have an interest, even a right, to control them.\par \par \uc2 SB1309\u8217\'20\'19s private cause of action is targeted, granting these relatives the right to sue providers only over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the notion that the parents, husbands, and in-laws of adult women have a legitimate legal claim to control those women\u8217\'20\'19s bodies and lives is one that could be expanded even beyond the question of reproductive choice. As heinous as the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban is, it may not stop there.\par \par \uc2 SB1309\u8217\'20\'19s conception of women as family possessions harkens back to ancient idea of women as the property of their fathers \u8211\'20\'13 property that could be transferred to other men in a transaction known as marriage. It is interesting that SB1309 also names fathers and their family members as having an interest in the woman\u8217\'20\'19s choices, implying that the right to control her and her body can be extended by inseminating her. If this all sounds baroque and morbid, like something out of the Old Testament, that\u8217\'20\'19s because it is: the Idaho bill was lobbied for by a group that calls itself the Idaho Family Policy Center, a non-profit dedicated to \u8220\'20\'1Cpromoting Biblically sound public policy\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In this way, Idaho and Texas seem somewhat at odds over what the justifying logic behind the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans of the post-Roe future should be. Are women\u8217\'20\'19s bodies public property, like Texas says, subject to regulation by anyone and everyone? Or are they private property, like in Idaho, subject to the more intimate terrorizing control of husbands, fathers, brothers and in-laws? It\u8217\'20\'19s not clear which vision will prevail. We can only wait for the next Republican-controlled state legislature to weigh in.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220325ei3p0020a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal