\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Texas  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ban forcing thousands to cross state lines for procedure \u8211\'20\'13 study\b0\par\par\uc2 Maya Yang \par \uc2 826 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 March 2022\par 11:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 An average of 1,400 Texas women traveled each month between September and December 2021 for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services\par \par \uc2 Texas\u8217\'20\'19s highly restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law has forced thousands of women to cross state lines to seek the procedure, according to new {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://sites.utexas.edu/txpep/files/2022/03/TxPEP-out-of-state-SB8.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  research}}}\uc2  by the University of Texas.\par \par \uc2 Since the passing of the law, known as Senate Bill 8 (SB8) last year {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/oct/03/sb8-south-texas-clinic-abortion-ban"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 banning almost all}}}\uc2  abortions in the state, an average of 1,400 Texas women traveled each month between September and December 2021 and sought \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services at 34 facilities in nearby states.\par \par \uc2 The average is approximately the same as the total number of Texans who traveled out of state each year to those clinics for all reasons between 2017 and 2019.\par \par \uc2 In August 2021, only 235 Texans went for abortions in the 34 facilities, the month {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/aug/30/abortion-law-federal-court-decision-texas"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 before SB8 took effect}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The states that many Texas residents sought abortions from include Arkansas, Colorado, Kansas, Louisiana, Mississippi, New Mexico and Oklahoma. Nearly three out of four Texans, or 45%, who traveled out of state during those months seeking the procedure obtained \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in Oklahoma, which just has four facilities that offer \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 According to the research, the number of Texans seen each month at these clinics since the law took effect is more than double the monthly average of all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients seen in Oklahoma in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Around one in four Texans seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or 27%, traveled to New Mexico to obtain such a service. The state has seven facilities. New Mexico does not require state-mandated counseling, waiting periods or parental consent for minors.\par \par \uc2 From October 2021 to February 2022, researchers interviewed 65 Texas women who sought \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services across state lines.\par \par \uc2 The participants ranged from 18 to 42 years old. Nearly half, or 46%, identified as Hispanic/Latinx, 23% as Black, 21% as white, 6% as Asian and 2% as more than one race. Participants reported a median gestational duration at \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  of nine weeks. Texas bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after about six weeks.\par \par \uc2 The participants described their frequent experiences with delays, often as a result of state law that include mandatory ultrasounds and counseling visits.\par \par \uc2 Some participants said they visited \u8220\'20\'1Cpregnancy resource centers\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 organizations that frequently offer ultrasounds free of charge but may seek to discourage pregnant people from seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Participants who had medical conditions that posed health risks for continuing their pregnancy said their healthcare providers were reluctant to offer information on out-of-state options for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 The law, which disproportionately affects communities of color, adds a burden in the need to travel, as not all may have the necessary resources to travel beyond state lines.\par \par \uc2 Due to long wait times at nearby \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services in neighboring states, many participants said they were unable to get an appointment at those facilities and had to travel even farther to obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 From Fort Worth, Texas, for example, the nearest Oklahoma facility is nearly 200 miles, or over three hours\u8217\'20\'19 drive away.\par \par \uc2 From Houston, the facility is 450 miles, or seven and a half hours away. To obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Oklahoma, people first must receive state-mandated counseling and then wait at least 72 hours, imposing a further cost and time burden that is beyond many would-be patients.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, unlike in New Mexico, minors are also required to notify a parent and obtain parental consent before receiving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 In June, the conservative-majority supreme court is expected to deliver a decision on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/nov/30/explainer-mississippi-abortion-rights-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization}}}\uc2 , the most significant \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights case since the landmark 1973 supreme court ruling in Roe v Wade, which effectively legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US.\par \par \uc2 The case pits Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s last \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic against the state\u8217\'20\'19s leadership, as the latter seeks to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks gestation and asks the highest court to overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve never felt more like the government doesn\u8217\'20\'19t give a shit about me than I do right now, to be honest with you. I\u8217\'20\'19ve never felt it so deep inside of me that I am so disposable, that I don\u8217\'20\'19t matter, that I don\u8217\'20\'19t get any bodily autonomy in such a horrible [life-threatening] situation \u8230\'20\'26 I just wish that I could have [had] done it here, at home,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://sites.utexas.edu/txpep/files/2022/03/TxPEP-out-of-state-SB8.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  said}}}\uc2  one Texas woman to the researchers of her experience in obtaining an out-of-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI really feel like this whole Texas law \u8211\'20\'13 I don\u8217\'20\'19t agree with it. It\u8217\'20\'19s not right, and it\u8217\'20\'19s so hard. I can just imagine the women who don\u8217\'20\'19t have the support system that I have, how hard it is for them to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if they\u8217\'20\'19re able to \u8230\'20\'26 If I didn\u8217\'20\'19t have my support system, it would have been so hard, if not impossible, to get this done,\u8221\'20\'1D another said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220309ei39001gv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
