\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 California considers allowing nurse practitioners to perform abortions without doctors\b0\par\par\uc2 Samira Sadeque \par \uc2 614 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 March 2022\par 18:09 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Bill in state senate would give more power to nurse practitioners to address looming staff shortages and potential increase in demand\par \par \uc2 California has introduced a bill that would allow nurse practitioners to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care without the supervision of a physician.\par \par \uc2 The San Diego senator \uc2 Toni Atkins\uc2 , who introduced the bill, said the expansion of nurse practitioners\u8217\'20\'19 roles would address both a looming shortage of healthcare professionals in the state, and help California prepare for a potential influx of patients driven by increasingly stringent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 Atkins said the measure would be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sd39.senate.ca.gov/news/20220302-senate-leader-atkins-introduces-legislation-expand-access-abortion-services-health"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 especially useful}}}\uc2  in serving communities of color that suffer most from understaffed healthcare systems.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen nurse practitioners are able to work independently, underserved communities \u8211\'20\'13 which are predominantly communities of color \u8211\'20\'13 benefit from an increase in access to services,\u8221\'20\'1D she added. \u8220\'20\'1C??SB 1375 would create opportunities for more Californians to receive the care they need, regardless of their zip code.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The bill SB 1375 was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202120220SB1375"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced}}}\uc2  on Wednesday, and would ease restrictions that were set in place by a 2013 law, which allowed nurse practitioners, certified midwives and physician assistants to carry out abortions as long as they were under the supervision of a physician and completed a certain set of requirements in their training. Under the new bill, nurse practitioners\u8217\'20\'19 who obtain a minimum number of hours and clinical experience could perform the procedure unsupervised.\par \par \uc2 Atkins said the goal is to increase the number of healthcare workers in California who can perform abortions ahead of a potential supreme court ruling this summer that could overturn Roe v Wade, allowing states to ban or severely restrict the procedure.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs states like Texas and others start to restrict further \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it just makes sense that women are going to find other places to go. California will be one of those states,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, indicating that the state will host women from places like Texas where the draconian \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2022/03/01/texas-abortion-law-supreme-court/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has been in effect for}}}\uc2  six months.\par \par \uc2 Atkins\u8217\'20\'19s measure will probably sit well in a state that has already branded itself {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/dec/08/california-sanctuary-abortion-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Ca sanctuary\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  for patients seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care while other states clamp down on.\par \par \uc2 In December, the California Future of \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Council announced a series of recommendations to consider to prepare for the high influx of prospective patients travelling from out of state that included paying for travel, lodging and access to care.\par \par \uc2 Atkins also cited a potential shortage of healthcare workers in the next decade and highlighted how this bill could address that.\par \par \uc2 According to a 2019 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://futurehealthworkforce.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/03/MeetingDemandForHealthFinalReportCFHWC.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  by the California Future Health Workforce Commission, the state is expecting to see a drop of 4,100 primary care clinicians and 600,000 homecare providers. The scarcity is being driven by a retiring generation of healthcare providers, at a time when health departments are struggling to recruit workers due to \u8220\'20\'1Clack of tools for recruiting, limited options for advancement, and instability of funded positions\u8221\'20\'1D, the report said.\par \par \uc2 One of the recommendations made in the report to address this shortage is to expand roles of nurse practitioners and to give them \u8220\'20\'1Cgreater practice authority\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFamilies who live in clinic deserts found throughout the state have a particularly difficult time accessing primary care, because there simply are not enough providers available to them,\u8221\'20\'1D Senator Atkins {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sd39.senate.ca.gov/news/20220302-senate-leader-atkins-introduces-legislation-expand-access-abortion-services-health"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CNurse practitioners can help fill that growing need.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Patti Gurney, President of the California Association for Nurse Practitioners (CANP), lauded the move and said they are sponsoring the bill.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is important that restrictions on practice be removed so that patients can receive timely, uninterrupted, and high-quality care in all health settings,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220304ei340025u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
