\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Blue states seek to protect  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights before supreme court decision\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 1,376 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 March 2022\par 10:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 An upcoming decision by the conservative-leaning court could overturn Roe v Wade and imperil \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights nationwide\par \par \uc2 In the face of imminent threats to legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US, lawmakers and activists in left-leaning \u8220\'20\'1Cblue\u8221\'20\'1D states are working to expand rights and access to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 State-level efforts to protect the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and reduce the cost of obtaining the procedure in states such as California, Rhode Island and Vermont are, in large part, a response to a forthcoming supreme court decision which could gut \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights nationally.\par \par \uc2 The landmark 1973 supreme court decision in Roe v Wade guaranteed pregnant people a constitutional right to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , invalidating dozens of state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that were in place at the time. However, many states never passed their own laws affirming a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2021/12/28/politics/the-year-supreme-court-conservatives-made-their-mark/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 newly conservative-leaning US supreme court}}}\uc2  looks willing to gut the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it prepares to decide on a new case, called {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/nov/30/explainer-mississippi-abortion-rights-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization}}}\uc2 . A decision in the case is expected in June.\par \par \uc2 If the court overturned Roe v Wade, it could return the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to states, many of which have not updated their laws in decades or are actively hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2021/12/06/1061896291/trigger-laws-are-abortion-bans-ready-to-go-if-roe-v-wade-is-overturned"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ready to}}}\uc2  enforce bans or severe restrictions.\par \par \uc2 In the event the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  reversed Roe v Wade, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2021/10/26-states-are-certain-or-likely-ban-abortion-without-roe-heres-which-ones-and-why"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 26 states}}}\uc2  would be certain or likely to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while just {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/state-policy/explore/abortion-policy-absence-roe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 15 states and Washington DC}}}\uc2  have laws to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CConservative legislators clearly feel they have the legal support to continue to pass \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and restrictions,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Elizabeth Nash\uc2 , interim associate director of state issues at the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a reproductive rights research center. Nash said researchers see \u8220\'20\'1Ca real trend around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, including comprehensive and early bans\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPart of the issue is we simply have fewer progressive state legislatures than we do conservative ones,\u8221\'20\'1D said Nash. However, she is optimistic about efforts to affirm \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and expand access in progressive states.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COver the past several years we have seen more action in state legislatures to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights than we had previously,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe look at 2019 \u8211\'20\'13 you had states from Illinois to Maine to Vermont and Rhode Island all looking to add statutory protections,\u8221\'20\'1D for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, momentum that has continued as threats to Roe v Wade have mounted.\par \par \uc2 Right-leaning lawmakers have long used \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rally their base, while protections conferred by Roe v Wade meant \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was often ranked as a low priority for left-leaning voters. As a result, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights legislation has languished for decades, even as rightwing politicians passed hundreds of restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Recent congressional efforts to codify the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in statute failed in the face of Republican opposition. Though the Democrat-led House of Representatives passed the Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Protection Act last year, which would have banned medically unnecessary restrictions on the procedure , the bill died in the Senate {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dallasnews.com/news/politics/2022/02/28/womens-health-protection-act-pushed-in-response-to-texas-new-abortion-law-fails-in-us-senate/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 this week}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The bill required 60 votes to overcome a filibuster. Democrats hold 50 seats, and Republicans universally opposed the measure. They were joined by one Democrat, Senator Joe Manchin of West Virginia.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe should have codified Roe in state law around the country,\u8221\'20\'1D said Liana Cassar, a Democratic state representative in Rhode Island. \u8220\'20\'1CBut there was a belief we weren\u8217\'20\'19t at risk \u8211\'20\'13 we weren\u8217\'20\'19t at risk of losing access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That has changed since Donald Trump was able to confirm three justices to the supreme court, tipping the nine-member bench firmly to the right.\par \par \uc2 Now, left-leaning lawmakers are seeking to pass more protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://newjerseymonitor.com/2022/01/13/murphy-signs-law-solidifying-abortion-rights-in-new-jersey/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New Jersey}}}\uc2  became the first state in the nation to pass an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related law in 2022, affirming the right to terminate a pregnancy and ordering a study of the barriers low-income women face to obtain the procedure.\par \par \uc2 In February, Vermont lawmakers voted to move forward on a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/02/08/abortion-roe-vermont/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 constitutional amendment}}}\uc2  to guarantee the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraception. The measure \u8211\'20\'13 the first of its kind in the US \u8211\'20\'13 will appear on the ballot in November, and voters in the state are expected to approve it.\par \par \uc2 Rhode Island affirmed a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in state law in 2019, but left in place restrictions that banned the state\u8217\'20\'19s employee health insurance and Medicaid, a health insurance program for the poor and disabled, from paying pay for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Lawmakers are now working to allow Medicaid to pay for abortions and other services.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a racism and a classism issue,\u8221\'20\'1D said Cassar, the sponsor of Rhode Island\u8217\'20\'19s legislation. If passed, Rhode Island would join 15 other states that allow Medicaid to pay for abortions.\par \par \uc2 In California, the Democratic governor, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , commissioned a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.plannedparenthoodaction.org/uploads/filer_public/d8/e1/d8e17825-72e0-4f6f-9c57-7549bb54261e/ca_fab_council_report_.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Future of \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  report, in part to examine the repercussions of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in neighboring states.\par \par \uc2 If the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  allows states to ban or severely restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://states.guttmacher.org/#california"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Guttmacher report}}}\uc2  found the number of out-of-state people who could find the nearest clinic in California could increase from 46,000 to 1.4 million overnight \u8211\'20\'13 a nearly 3,000% increase.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFolks can fall into the thinking that advocacy, aggressive advocacy, to expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is not necessary,\u8221\'20\'1D said Onyemma C Obiekea, policy analyst for the Black Women for Wellness Action Project, a reproductive justice group which helped steer the Future of \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  committee. However, potentially devastating supreme court rulings have, \u8220\'20\'1Creally shifted that\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 One of the first recommendations lawmakers are tackling from the report would aim to further protect California residents from the high cost of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . California\u8217\'20\'19s Medicaid program already pays for abortions. A new bill would end out-of-pocket costs for people who seek abortions and have private insurance.\par \par \uc2 That could reduce the cost of terminating a pregnancy by hundreds of dollars for about 9,600 of the 23,000 California women who seek abortions in the state each year, reducing the number of people who face high costs by 41%, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://analyses.chbrp.com/document/view.php?id=1542"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 California Health Benefits Review Program}}}\uc2 . The bill would only impact people whose private health insurance is regulated by the state. The health insurance plans of about 5.7 million Californians are regulated by the federal government.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps the most ambitious efforts to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are taking place in so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cpurple states\u8221\'20\'1D such as Michigan, where \u8211\'20\'13 like Vermont \u8211\'20\'13 a coalition of reproductive rights groups are pushing a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/policy/healthcare/588731-michigan-group-launches-petition-for-ballot-initiative-to-explicitly-affirm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ballot initiative}}}\uc2  to provide a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution. Currently, if the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  overturned Roe v Wade, it could allow a 1931 law banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be reinstated. To get a question on the 2022 ballot Michigan groups need to collect more than 425,000 signatures, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/local/michigan/2022/01/07/group-launches-petition-drive-explicitly-affirm-abortion-rights-michigan/9128200002/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Detroit News}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Michigan has Democratic governor but \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights legislation has no realistic chance of moving through the Republican-controlled statehouse, which prompted a drive to put the question to voters. The chances of the initiative succeeding are unclear. An initiative to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ballotpedia.org/Michigan_Abortion_Legalization_Initiative,_Proposal_B_(1972)"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 legalize \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  in Michigan}}}\uc2  failed in 1972, just one year before Roe v Wade was decided.\par \par \uc2 In all states, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/nov/30/abortion-rulings-history-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 powerful opposition to efforts to expand \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights}}}\uc2  and access has come from Christian anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations, the Catholic church and aligned Republicans. In just one example, the Catholic church has been among the most vocal opposition to Cassar\u8217\'20\'19s bill in Rhode Island.\par \par \uc2 Even if more progressive states pass and expand access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , millions people in states hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would face insurmountable challenges to obtaining a legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/03/texas-abortion-ban-six-months-grave-warning"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 scenario is currently playing out in Texas}}}\uc2 , where state lawmakers have successfully enacted a law that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before most women know they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 States like California are just \u8220\'20\'1Ctoo far away\u8221\'20\'1D for many of the people who live in conservative southern states that are most likely to pass harsh \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, said Michelle Anderson, state organizer with the Texas-based and Black women-focused reproductive rights justice group the Afiya Center.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe bottom line is that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans do not stop abortions,\u8221\'20\'1D said Anderson. \u8220\'20\'1CThey just make abortions less safe, and this is especially true for Black women.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220304ei34001jm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
