\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Majority of US abortions in 2020 were medication abortions, analysis finds\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 780 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 February 2022\par 16:21 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The increase came as telehealth and mailing medication during the pandemic made healthcare accessible for vulnerable people\par \par \uc2 Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  accounted for the majority of all abortions for the first time in US history in 2020, a new analysis by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2022/02/medication-abortion-now-accounts-more-half-all-us-abortions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Guttmacher Institute}}}\uc2  has shown.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes called the \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill\u8221\'20\'1D, medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a two-drug treatment that is approved to terminate a pregnancy up to 10 weeks gestation or less. The drugs, sold under the brand name Mifeprex, have accounted for an increasingly large share of all abortions in the US since its introduction in 2000.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, the proportion of all pregnancies terminated with medication in the US jumped to 54% from 39% in 2017. Experts believe the increase was driven by the Covid pandemic and updated \uc2 FDA\uc2  regulations, which allowed the medication to be prescribed via telemedicine.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are seeing more people using medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and some of that may have to do with the pandemic and being able to protect themselves from Covid,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Elizabeth Nash\uc2 , interim associate director of state issues at the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a leading reproductive rights group.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAccessing medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  means [people] don\u8217\'20\'19t have to travel long distances, take time off of work and arrange for childcare,\u8221\'20\'1D said Nash. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s important too\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s more, pandemic-era regulations that allowed for telehealth prescribing and mailing of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication meant many people did not have to face aggressive protesters or harassment outside clinics.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBecause so many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients are low-income, are Black and brown individuals, are young people, are LGBTQ individuals \u8211\'20\'13 people who have been systematically oppressed and faced barriers to accessing healthcare \u8211\'20\'13 being able to get access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through telehealth is incredibly important,\u8221\'20\'1D said Nash.\par \par \uc2 Growing use of the \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill\u8221\'20\'1D is also seen as a threat by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigners, who have pushed state laws restricting access to the medication and made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication the subject of online disinformation campaigns. Such campaigners would not be able to confront people seeking abortions if they are not forced to go to clinics.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigners have been emboldened by a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/dec/03/roe-v-wade-abortion-roll-back-democracy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 forthcoming supreme court decision}}}\uc2 , which is expected to severely restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights by striking at the landmark 1973 Roe v Wade ruling, which found a constitutional right to terminate a pregnancy and invalidated dozens of state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans nationally. A decision is expected in June.\par \par \uc2 The findings about medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are part of a larger set of research conducted by Guttmacher. Every three years, the institute surveys all known abortions providers in the US. The survey is not expected to conclude until later 2022. The estimated proportion of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was derived from an early analysis, and is not expected to fall below 50% even when all survey data is in.\par \par \uc2 Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is regarded as very safe, and uses mifepristone, followed by misoprostol. The drugs were introduced in 2000. By 2016, more than 3 million American women had taken the drug with 19 deaths reported, an effective mortality rate of 0.00063%. By contrast, giving birth poses a risk of death 14-times higher, according to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMsb1612526"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New England Journal of Medicine commentary}}}\uc2  article (the author received an unrestricted grant from Danco Laboratories, the maker of Mifeprex).\par \par \uc2 In April 2020, as the pandemic shut down healthcare facilities across the country, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  temporarily allowed telemedicine prescribing and mailing of mifepristone. The decision was made permanent in December.\par \par \uc2 In 2022, 16 states have so far introduced restrictions on mifepristone, including seven states which have introduced legislation to outlaw medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  entirely.\par \par \uc2 Thirty-two states already limit the healthcare providers who can prescribe medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to doctors, even as providers such as nurse practitioners may also manage patient\u8217\'20\'19s health. In 19 states, the prescriber must be present at the time mifepristone is administered, effectively barring telemedicine prescribing.\par \par \uc2 In addition to legal restrictions, medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become the subject of disinformation campaigns by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations, many of whom advertise \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2019/jul/25/revealed-doctor-reverse-abortion-trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  reversal}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D protocols.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2  has described so-called \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  reversal \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.acog.org/advocacy/facts-are-important/medication-abortion-reversal-is-not-supported-by-science"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unproven and unethical}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. The only high-quality, randomized controlled trial to be conducted on the treatment was halted in 2019 when three women {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31809439/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 suffered severe hemorrhaging}}}\uc2  and had to be hospitalized.\par \par \uc2 Guttmacher released a second analysis showing it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2022/02/financial-instability-and-delays-access-sexual-and-reproductive-health-care-due"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 became significantly harder}}}\uc2  for low-income women to access birth control during the pandemic. A three-state survey in Arizona, Iowa and Wisconsin found between 30% and more than half of women faced delays or barriers in obtaining contraception. Those obstacles were exacerbated when women faced financial hardship caused by the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220224ei2o00232\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
