\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Texas law causes abortions to drop by 60% as people seek care in other states\b0\par\par\uc2 Guardian staff and agency \par \uc2 517 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 February 2022\par 00:42 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 State data showed procedures went from 5,400 to 2,200 in the month after the near-total ban was enacted in September\par \par \uc2 Abortions performed by registered providers in Texas fell by 60% in the first month after the state passed the most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law in the US in decades, according to new figures.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 2,200 abortions were reported by Texas providers in September after a new law took effect that bans the procedure once cardiac activity is detected, usually around six weeks of pregnancy and when many do not know they are pregnant. The law is without exception in cases of rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 The figures were released this month by the Texas health and human services commission. In August, there had been more than 5,400 abortions statewide. State health officials said more data will be released on a monthly basis.\par \par \uc2 The numbers offer a fuller picture of the sharp drop in patients that Texas doctors have described in their clinics over the past five months, during which time courts have repeatedly allowed the restrictions to stay in place. It has left some Texas patients traveling hundreds of miles to clinics in neighboring states or farther, causing a backlog of appointments in those places.\par \par \uc2 People seeking legal abortions have traveled to Oklahoma, New Mexico, Colorado, Kansas, Arkansas and Louisiana, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dallasnews.com/news/2021/10/02/for-texas-women-complicated-journey-to-get-an-abortion-leads-to-shreveport-clinic/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reporting}}}\uc2  by the Dallas Morning News .\par \par \uc2 The first month after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/SB8?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #SB8}}}\uc2  took effect, abortions in Texas dropped almost 60% \u8211\'20\'13 not because people stopped needing care.\par \par \uc2 Instead, Texans started traveling 300+ miles to out-of-state clinics, which struggle to keep up with demand.\par \par \uc2 Abolish SB8. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/tLokTzKX9m"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/tLokTzKX9m}}}\uc2  \u8212\'20\'14 Women's March (@womensmarch) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/womensmarch/status/1491535129969315840?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 February 9, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 The Texas law conflicts with the landmark \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  ruling Roe v Wade, and other rulings, that prevent a state from banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  early in pregnancy, but was written in a way that has essentially outmaneuvered those precedents.\par \par \uc2 Under the law, any private citizen is entitled to make a claim for $10,000 or more by bringing a lawsuit against someone who performed or helped a woman obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the limit \u8211\'20\'13 which opponents have condemned as a bounty. So far, no anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  supporters have filed any suits.\par \par \uc2 Texas Right to Life, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, issued a statement saying \u8220\'20\'1Cthe success of the Texas Heartbeat Act is embodied by every child saved\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers have acknowledged the law is likely to stay on the books for the foreseeable future.\par \par \uc2 It comes as the supreme court has signaled a willingness to weaken or reverse the Roe v Wade precedent in a ruling that is expected in June.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in more liberal states, such as California, Illinois and New York, are expanding clinics and boosting travel assistance to prepare for an influx of patients from conservative states if the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  does end or severely undermines the constitutional right to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Associated Press contributed to this report\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220210ei2a003ph\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
