\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Tens of thousands \u8216\'20\'18march for life\u8217\'20\'19 in Washington as fate of Roe v Wade looms\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Gambino in Washington \par \uc2 1,278 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 January 2022\par 02:52 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative supermajority appears open to reversing Roe, overturning nearly 50 years of precedent since 1973 decision\par \par \uc2 In 1974, on the first anniversary of the Roe v Wade supreme court decision, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents gathered on the National Mall in Washington to \u8220\'20\'1Cmarch for life\u8221\'20\'1D. They vowed to return each year until the ruling, which established the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was no longer the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists from across the country braved sub-zero temperatures and the coronavirus pandemic to assemble in Washington, more hopeful than ever that this would be their last march to a court where the fate of Roe will soon be decided.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are hoping and praying that this year, 2022, will bring a historic change for life,\u8221\'20\'1D Jeanne Mancini, president of the March for Life Education and Defense Fund, told a crowd tens of thousands strong and waving signs that read \u8220\'20\'1CI am the post-Roe generation\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1CThe future is anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Praising followers for standing against what she called the \u8220\'20\'1Csingle-most critical rights abuse of our time\u8221\'20\'1D, Mancini said they sent a clear message to the supreme court: \u8220\'20\'1CRoe is not settled law.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Chris Smith, a Republican congressman from New Jersey, described the mood as one of \u8220\'20\'1Cfresh hope and heightened expectations\u8221\'20\'1D. The court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative supermajority appears open to reversing Roe, thereby overturning nearly 50 years of precedent since the 1973 decision.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s optimism in the air, there is a sense that a significant hurdle to protect the unborn is about to move,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 The rally took place a day before the 49th anniversary of the Roe decision. The theme of the march, also in its 49th year, was \u8220\'20\'1Cequality begins in the womb.\u8221\'20\'1D Speakers told rally-goers that their cause was bound up with the struggles for racial justice and gender equality and described \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as \u8220\'20\'1Cthe ultimate form of discrimination\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The march typically draws about 100,000 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents by the busload to Washington. But this year\u8217\'20\'19s march took place amid a wave of Omicron infections in the nation\u8217\'20\'19s capital that limited turnout.\par \par \uc2 Some activists said on social media they would not attend because of a new mandate in Washington requiring anyone over the age of 12 to show vaccination proof before entering restaurants, conference centers and other public places.\par \par \uc2 Still, the event attracted a large and enthusiastic crowd, priests, pastors and busloads of high school students, among them. Together after the rally they marched to the supreme court singing hymns and chanting \u8220\'20\'1CHey hey, ho ho, Roe v Wade has got to go!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The jubilant demonstration comes as the supreme court reviews a case involving a Mississippi law which bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks of pregnancy, a direct challenge to Roe. At oral arguments, several members of the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative bloc appeared open to not only upholding the ban, but to overruling Roe entirely. A decision is expected by the end of June, months before the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve been building to this moment,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Victoria Cobb\uc2 , president of the Family Foundation of Virginia who spoke on a virtual panel organized by the March. Her group was active in helping confirm Amy Coney Barrett to the supreme court, part of a decades-long legal strategy by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents to remake the nation\u8217\'20\'19s federal court system.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe heard justices say that precedent shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be upheld if it was incorrectly decided in the first place,\u8221\'20\'1D Cobb said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s a big deal.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Several Republican lawmakers appeared on stage and virtually to voice their unwavering support for the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cause, declaring that the movement was \u8220\'20\'1Cwinning this battle\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Last year, states enacted more than 100 new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, a record, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research organization that supports reproductive rights and tracks state-level legislation. The group has called 2021 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://the/%20Worst%20Year%20for%20Abortion%20Rights%20in%20Almost%20Half%20a%20Century"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cthe worst year for \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights in almost half a century}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D, and estimates that 26 US states are \u8220\'20\'1Ccertain or likely\u8221\'20\'1D to immediately ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if Roe is overturned.\par \par \uc2 And conservative states are already laying the groundwork for new restrictions as fights over issues like telemedicine and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills gain momentum.\par \par \uc2 Though the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement has made significant legal and policy gains in recent decades, public opinion polls have consistently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/politics/2021/11/09/beyond-red-vs-blue-the-political-typology-2/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found}}}\uc2  that a majority of Americans believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or some circumstances.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf Roe falls, the battle lines will change,\u8221\'20\'1D Mancini said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut make no mistake, the fight for life will need to continue in the states.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kelly and Greg King, a married couple from Los Angeles, who were attending the event for a third time, said the supreme court decision would likely push their state to expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. They worry about plans to make California a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-california-sanctuary-625a118108bcda253196697c83548d5b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sanctuary}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D for out-of-state patients seeking reproductive care in a post-Roe legal landscape.\par \par \uc2 Clear-eyed about the state\u8217\'20\'19s progressive politics, Kelly King said she would focus her efforts on \u8220\'20\'1Cchanging hearts\u8221\'20\'1D rather than changing policy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become \u8230\'20\'26 \u8221\'20\'1D Kelly King said, searching for the word. \u8220\'20\'1CNormalized,\u8221\'20\'1D her husband chimed in. \u8220\'20\'1CYes, normalized,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s the problem.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hours before marchers arrived on the National Mall, the supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jan/21/supreme-court-declines-to-speed-challenge-texas-abortion-limits"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 declined}}}\uc2  to accelerate a legal challenge to a Texas law that has effectively banned abortions in the second-largest state.\par \par \uc2 Yet among the speakers, there were few references to that victory or to the Texas law, which is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2021/10/04/1042454835/the-provisions-in-texas-restrictive-abortion-law-are-not-popular-an-npr-poll-fin"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deeply unpopular}}}\uc2 , including among Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Pro-choice supporters also marked the anniversary, using the occasion to \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.shaheen.senate.gov/news/press/shaheen-statement-ahead-of-roe-v-wade-anniversary"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sound the alarm\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  on the threat posed to reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 Mini Timmaraju, the president of NARAL Pro-Choice America, warned that a \u8220\'20\'1Csmall but vocal minority\u8221\'20\'1D was \u8220\'20\'1Cdetermined to undermine the will of the majority of people in this country who support reproductive freedom\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey falsely claim to be supporters of \u8216\'20\'18equality\u8217\'20\'19 all while working ardently to block \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and end the legal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D she said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CMake no mistake \u8211\'20\'13 this movement\u8217\'20\'19s end goals would only criminalize and endanger people based on pregnancy outcomes, furthering inequality.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At the White House, press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  noted the anniversary of Roe v Wade during her press briefing, saying that \u8220\'20\'1Creproductive healthcare has been under extreme and relentless assault ever since, especially in recent months\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 She said the Biden administration was committed to working with Congress {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/24/house-democrats-abortion-federal-right-vote"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  to pass federal legislation}}}\uc2  essentially enshrining into law a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Democratic-controlled House passed the bill last year, but it remains stalled in the Senate, where it faces a Republican filibuster.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re deeply committed to making sure everyone has access to care and we will defend it with every tool we have,\u8221\'20\'1D Psaki said.\par \par \uc2 At the rally, the presence of Make America Great Again hats was a reminder of the mutually beneficial relationship forged between Christian conservatives and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who became {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/jan/24/trump-march-for-life-washington-anti-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the first sitting president to attend the event}}}\uc2  in 2020.\par \par \uc2 As they gathered, Trump voiced his allegiance: \u8220\'20\'1CAs you gather together today for the March for Life, I am with you in spirit!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 While many of the speakers anticipated the end of Roe, several demonstrators said they would continue to attend future marches until its mission \u8220\'20\'1Cto make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  unthinkable\u8221\'20\'1D was achieved.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI just pray every year that this is the last year we\u8217\'20\'19re here,\u8221\'20\'1D said Janice LePage, who works for the youth ministry in the Archdiocese of St Louis. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m praying that the following year will be a march of celebration.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220121ei1l003eb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
