\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We\u8217\'20\'19re not backing down\u8217\'20\'19: the Texas church fighting for  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights\b0\par\par\uc2 Mary Tuma in Austin \par \uc2 2,043 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 December 2021\par 08:35 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In the face of a draconian \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in effect for more than three months, the mission has only grown stronger for a progressive congregation\par \par \uc2 In the late 60s, the burgeoning movement to legalize US \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  state by state found an unlikely yet loyal ally \u8211\'20\'13 a contingent of women at the First Unitarian Universalist church in Dallas, Texas.\par \par \uc2 In lieu of knitting sessions and bake sales, the church\u8217\'20\'19s Women\u8217\'20\'19s Alliance advocated for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and even had a hand in legally supporting Roe v Wade, the pivotal \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  case that protects \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in the US as a constitutional right.\par \par \uc2 The trailblazing women laid the groundwork for not only the church\u8217\'20\'19s intrepid commitment to reproductive freedom over the decades, but today\u8217\'20\'19s growing pro-choice faith community movement in Texas.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese women had the foresight at the time to understand that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights wasn\u8217\'20\'19t just a debate for the moment but would be very important to future generations of women,\u8221\'20\'1D says Kathleen Campbell, current president of the Women\u8217\'20\'19s Alliance.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey also understood that one of the core teachings of the church is the belief that every human is worthy of love and dignity and that a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to control her own body and reproductive future is a very natural extension of that principle.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Dallas church has carried on their legacy for generations by openly supporting reproductive rights groups and causes, spiritually advising \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients, and even helping challenge onerous \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in court.\par \par \uc2 Even as Texas\u8217\'20\'19s leadership has become more conservative and the voices of anti-choice evangelical religious figures became louder, the church remained dedicated to its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocacy.\par \par \uc2 Now, in the face of a draconian {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/01/texas-abortion-law-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban}}}\uc2  in effect for more than three months \u8211\'20\'13 and the prospect of the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  potentially {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/dec/02/supreme-court-signalling-end-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturning}}}\uc2  Roe v Wade next year \u8211\'20\'13 that mission has only grown stronger for the progressive congregation, who hope others around the US will join their movement.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We believe God loves us all\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Amid the feminist revolution, the Alliance grappled with what a woman\u8217\'20\'19s role in society should \u8211\'20\'13 and could \u8211\'20\'13 be, and a large part of that conversation included reproductive freedom. In 1969 the organization held study groups over the next year to educate themselves on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures and laws; they heard from members of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , doctors and lawyers.\par \par \uc2 At first they treaded conservatively into the issue, but soon became \u8220\'20\'1Cpassionate\u8221\'20\'1D about pro-choice values, according to a source closely involved at the time. They examined the moral, legal, and practical reasons to legalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care and began organizing, making trips to the Texas capitol to advocate for a bill that would repeal the state\u8217\'20\'19s anti-choice law, which only made an exception in the event the woman\u8217\'20\'19s life was in danger.\par \par \uc2 As largely privileged white women in a conservative city, the group was also very aware that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely be accessible for them and were motivated by the fact that legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care would be difficult, if not impossible, for low-income women and many women of color, a reality that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2019/may/19/abortion-ban-alabama-women-of-color-poor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 persists today}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Jane Roe\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 In 1970, Linda Coffee, an attorney and friend of the church, introduced the women to Dallas resident Norma McCorvey \u8211\'20\'13 known widely under the pseudonym \u8220\'20\'1CJane Roe\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 and told them she was about to launch a legal challenge against local district attorney Henry Wade.\par \par \uc2 Wanting to assist in any way they could, the women took the bold step of filing a legal brief on behalf of McCorvey in her pursuit to strike down the Texas law, helping spur support for what would later become Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 Over the decades the church has carried on the tradition of ardently supporting reproductive rights, a seemingly radical mission in a state headed by Christian rightwing leaders deeply hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe see that moment many years ago not just as a shining era to look back on, but as a charge for us to go forward and continue to work to support reproductive rights with any opportunity in our particular moment of history,\u8221\'20\'1D says Campbell. \u8220\'20\'1CTo this day, their contribution resonates, as we still very much support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Among its activism the church offers comprehensive sex education, financial support to \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds like the Texas Equal Access Fund, and volunteer to drive and safely escort patients to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics.\par \par \uc2 While so many think all religious people are against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that\u8217\'20\'19s just not true\par \par \uc2 The Rev Daniel Kanter\par \par \uc2 The church also has a strong presence within an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic itself. The Rev Daniel Kanter, senior minister and chief executive of First Unitarian church of Dallas, started a multi-faith rotating chaplaincy at Southwestern Women\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic five years ago. Kanter, who has served on the board of \uc2 Planned Parenthood Federation of America\uc2 , and other faith leaders volunteer their time listening and spiritually guiding patients in the waiting rooms. They\u8217\'20\'19ve heard heartbreaking stories about abuse and rape; about fetal anomalies that would kill the mother if carried to term; about pregnant women with children they already couldn\u8217\'20\'19t support or care for. Rather than judgment, Kanter meets them with an open heart and compassion.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile so many think all religious people are against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that\u8217\'20\'19s just not true,\u8221\'20\'1D says Kanter. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are there to support those who have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we believe God loves us all and we shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be silenced or shamed for the decisions we make about our bodies.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 And like its feminist predecessors decades ago, the church has not shied away from getting involved in major \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases: Kanter is a plaintiff in the legal challenge to the state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/may/19/texas-abortion-ban-law-greg-abbott"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Senate Bill 8}}}\uc2 . The law, which went into effect on 1 September, has devastated reproductive health care in the second largest state in the US for three months. The \uc2 US supreme court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2021/dec/10/supreme-court-texas-abortion-ban-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 failed to block}}}\uc2  SB 8 earlier this month, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2021/12/16/texas-abortion-law-legal-fight/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sent}}}\uc2  the legal challenge from Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to the conservative US fifth circuit court of appeals. The law allows for any private individual to sue anyone who \u8220\'20\'1Caids or abets\u8221\'20\'1D \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care beyond around six weeks of pregnancy, casting a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/15/no-one-wants-to-get-sued-some-abortion-providers-have-stopped-working-in-texas"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 chilling effect}}}\uc2  on those who seek to assist \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients, including clergy. SB 8, says Kanter, is an affront to religious liberty.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSB 8 censors what clergy can say in the confines of a private counseling room,\u8221\'20\'1D says Kanter. \u8220\'20\'1CAll faith leaders of all religions should be concerned by this.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Taken God hostage\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 With its long history of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights support, it may be no wonder then that the progressive church is leading the Just Texas {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://justtx.org/rfc/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Reproductive Freedom Congregation}}}\uc2  movement, which launched this August. The first to join, the Dallas church and 29 others across Texas are working to remove the stigma associated with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I have no doubt in my mind God is on our side and that God respects and trusts women\par \par \uc2 The Rev Erika Forbes\par \par \uc2 The movement, started by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tfn.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Texas Freedom Network}}}\uc2 , which was founded in 1995 by former Planned Parenthood president Cecile Richards, seeks to unite faith groups to commit to three tenets: trusting and respecting women; ensuring congregants are free from shaming people\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive decisions; and believing access to comprehensive, affordable health services is a moral and social good. Congregants learn to testify at the state capitol, publish op-eds, attend marches, help \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic staff and engage in other forms of activism.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are on a quest to wrestle God back from the religious pro-life conservatives who have taken God hostage,\u8221\'20\'1D says the Rev Erika Forbes, faith and outreach manager for Just Texas. \u8220\'20\'1CI have no doubt in my mind God is on our side and that God respects and trusts women.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Forbes has spiritually counseled dozens of Texas women through their abortions. She says her own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  experiences as a teenager \u8220\'20\'1Csaved\u8221\'20\'1D her life and motivated her to pursue her calling, ministry. She spreads the message that it is a \u8220\'20\'1Cdivine right\u8221\'20\'1D for a person to choose to control their bodies and futures without shame or stigma.\par \par \uc2 The Reproductive Freedom Congregation movement plans to expand nationally in January, says Forbes. At least 50 congregations across the country have expressed interest in joining. The goal is to create a \u8220\'20\'1Clasting culture change\u8221\'20\'1D that subverts the dominant narrative that claims people of faith don\u8217\'20\'19t support reproductive freedom.\par \par \uc2 That narrative often feels ingrained in US \u8211\'20\'13 and Texas \u8211\'20\'13 culture, in large part due to religious right\u8217\'20\'19s success in tying anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies to Christian family values and sexual morality, an idea boosted by the Moral Majority movement of the late 70s and 80s.\par \par \uc2 However, researchers stress that narrative is a largely false notion, or at least one that is far more complex. The religious right \u8211\'20\'13 mostly composed of conservative Catholics and evangelicals \u8211\'20\'13 carry an \u8220\'20\'1Coutsized\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cdisproportionate\u8221\'20\'1D influence in the overall religious community when it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to their strong organizing power and are not necessarily reflective of all Christian groups.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is certainly an issue that catalyzed the Christian right, Christian conservatives don\u8217\'20\'19t all sort neatly into being anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s a lot more granular than that,\u8221\'20\'1D says Amanda Hollis-Brusky, associate professor and chair of politics at Pomona College who studies the Christian right.\par \par \uc2 Some 55% of Catholics believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases, as do 63% of non-evangelical white Protestants and, notably, 21% of white evangelicals, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewforum.org/fact-sheet/public-opinion-on-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pew Research Center}}}\uc2  study found earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Frederick Clarkson, senior analyst with social justice think tank Political Research Associates and author of Dispatches from the Religious Left: The Future of Faith and Politics in America , has followed the Christian right for the past four decades. While Roe v Wade fueled the Christian right\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Csuccessful efforts\u8221\'20\'1D to mobilize and enmesh themselves in Republican electoral politics, that approach has resulted in a \u8220\'20\'1Cshrinking minority\u8221\'20\'1D led by white evangelical Protestants exercising electoral power \u8220\'20\'1Cdisproportionate\u8221\'20\'1D to their numbers.\par \par \uc2 Clarkson believes that the Christian right could be \u8220\'20\'1Cdethroned\u8221\'20\'1D by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://politicalresearch.org/2020/09/28/annotated-directory-prochoice-religious-community-united-states"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 vast pro-choice faith community}}}\uc2  that comprises the majority or at least, near-majority, of the overall religious community in the US; it is akin to a sleeping giant waiting to be \u8220\'20\'1Creckoned\u8221\'20\'1D with.\par \par \uc2 As \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are in the crosshairs nationally after the Supreme Court recently heard Dobbs v Jackson \u8211\'20\'13 a challenge to Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s 15-week ban that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/dec/01/oral-arguments-mississippi-abortion-case-begin-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 could upend}}}\uc2  Roe \u8211\'20\'13 the time to mobilize feels especially urgent, says Clarkson.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf the pro-choice religious community in the US could become as organized as the Christian right and electorally focused it would be a sea change in American culture and politics,\u8221\'20\'1D says Clarkson. \u8220\'20\'1CThey have the potential power to erode \u8211\'20\'13 or even end \u8211\'20\'13 the Christian right\u8217\'20\'19s theocratic ambitions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Often overshadowed by the Christian right, Clarkson points to the long history of religious groups \u8211\'20\'13 including mainline Protestant denominations, Catholics and most of Judaism \u8211\'20\'13 supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. For instance, the Clergy Consultation Service on \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  comprised of 2,000 religious leaders in the late 60s was the largest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referral service in the US before Roe, helping thousands of women access safe care. Today, organizations like the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://rcrc.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Religious Coalition for Reproductive Choice}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.catholicsforchoice.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Catholics for Choice}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 both founded in 1973 following Roe \u8211\'20\'13 have continued to gain influence.\par \par \uc2 Of course, challenging the loudest religious voices in the room comes with its share of hostile pushback, especially in Texas. Kanter and his congregation must face vocal anti-choice protesters who rally in front of the church, as well as dozens of phone calls and letters ranging from moderate anger from fellow Christians to \u8220\'20\'1Cextremely violent and threatening\u8221\'20\'1D messages, some of which Kanter has been forced to report to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile our views have caused some friction, we are not afraid and not backing down,\u8221\'20\'1D says Kanter. \u8220\'20\'1CWe want to steer the wheel back from evangelicals and those who claim to be pro-life. We feel strong and more ready than ever to do so.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 22 December 2021. The senior analyst\u8217\'20\'19s name is Frederick Clarkson, not Carlson.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211220ehck000xe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
