\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 How dismantling Roe v Wade could imperil other \u8216\'20\'18core, basic human rights\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 1,217 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 December 2021\par 22:44 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Supreme court appears inclined to severely curtail or overturn Roe v Wade after hearing Mississippi case, which could affect gay rights, contraceptives and fertility treatments\par \par \uc2 Constitutional scholars in the US said a litany of rights, from same-sex marriage and sex to birth control and in vitro fertilization, could come into question if the country\u8217\'20\'19s highest court moves to overrule or weaken Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 The supreme court last week heard arguments in the case Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, which centers on whether the state of Mississippi can ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 15 weeks gestation, roughly nine weeks before bans are permitted under current law.\par \par \uc2 The Mississippi case is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/nov/30/explainer-mississippi-abortion-rights-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 widely regarded}}}\uc2  as the most important \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights case since Roe v Wade, when the supreme court effectively legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationally in 1973. A decision in the Dobbs case is expected June 2022.\par \par \uc2 Although supreme court opinions are notoriously difficult to predict, a majority of justices on the conservative-leaning court appeared {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/dec/01/oral-arguments-mississippi-abortion-case-begin-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 inclined to severely curtail or overturn Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 , which protects \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in states hostile to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Legal scholars warned that the impacts of such a move would likely be widespread, because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are rooted in the same implied constitutional right to privacy that is the foundation for other intimate personal decisions Americans now take for granted.\par \par \uc2 Gay rights, contraceptives, certain fertility treatments and even interracial marriage, \u8220\'20\'1Care imperiled because they\u8217\'20\'19re all rooted in that right to privacy\u8221\'20\'1D, Melissa Murray, a law professor at New York University law school and an expert in constitutional, family and reproductive rights law, told \uc2 the Guardian\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAll of this has been implied because they\u8217\'20\'19re understood to be core, basic human rights,\u8221\'20\'1D said Murray. \u8220\'20\'1CYou don\u8217\'20\'19t need the state to recognize them because they are vested in you by virtue of being a human.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Currently, states are prevented from banning abortions before a fetus can survive outside the womb, a concept known as \u8220\'20\'1Cviability\u8221\'20\'1D. But at the hearing on 1 December, a majority of justices appeared ready to uphold Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s law, which would require either invalidating the \u8220\'20\'1Cviability\u8221\'20\'1D standard or overturn Roe v Wade entirely.\par \par \uc2 In arguments, justices pointed to several ways they may reinterpret the Roe v Wade decision. Some, such as Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcripts/2021/19-1392_4425.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 skeptical there is a right to privacy}}}\uc2  and were swayed by the lack of an explicit reference to the right in the constitution, a concept known as \u8220\'20\'1Ctextualism\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf we were talking about the second amendment, I know exactly what we\u8217\'20\'19re talking about,\u8221\'20\'1D said Thomas. \u8220\'20\'1CIf we\u8217\'20\'19re talking about the fourth amendment, I know what we\u8217\'20\'19re talking about because it\u8217\'20\'19s written. It\u8217\'20\'19s there.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That argument could be paired with one pushed forward by conservatives, such as Mississippi solicitor general Scott Stewart, who argued a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not grounded in the \u8220\'20\'1Chistory or tradition\u8221\'20\'1D of the country.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  [is] not grounded in the text,\u8221\'20\'1D said Stewart. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s grounded on abstract concepts that this court has rejected in other contexts as supplying a substantive right\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The theory underlying that right to privacy is called \u8220\'20\'1Csubstantive due process\u8221\'20\'1D, or the doctrine that the constitution protects both the procedures of due process, such as how criminal law is applied, and \u8220\'20\'1Csubstantive\u8221\'20\'1D guarantees of life, liberty and property.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you ask where rights come from in the US constitution there\u8217\'20\'19s basically two general answers,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mary Ziegler, a law professor at Florida State University and a historian who has studied \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights since Roe v Wade. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s the rights spelled out in the text of the constitution,\u8221\'20\'1D such as rights to bear arms or against unreasonable search and seizure, \u8220\'20\'1Cand there\u8217\'20\'19s other rights, like the right to marry and the right to parent that are not in the text of the constitution\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Those are rights established by substantive due process. For example, in 1965 the court struck down birth control bans for married couples in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2012/03/02/opinion/contraception-war-goes-on.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Griswold v Connecticut}}}\uc2 . In 1967 with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.oyez.org/cases/1966/395"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Loving v Virginia}}}\uc2 , the court invalidated anti-miscegenation laws that barred interracial marriage. In 1972 in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.oyez.org/cases/1971/70-17"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Eisenstadt v Baird}}}\uc2 , the court found people who were not married also had a right to birth control. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/nov/30/abortion-rulings-history-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 In 1973}}}\uc2 , the court recognized a right to terminate a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese rights of parental autonomy are underpinnings of the right to privacy; marriage is included in this,\u8221\'20\'1D said Murray. \u8220\'20\'1CIn a later case, the state says marriage [and] procreation are basic civil rights of man.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Cases based in substantive due process continued into the modern era, when in 2003 the court invalidated anti-sodomy statutes in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.oyez.org/cases/2002/02-102"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Lawrence v Texas}}}\uc2  and established a right to same-sex conduct. In 2016, the court found same-sex couples also had a right to marry in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.oyez.org/cases/2014/14-556"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Obergefell v Hodges}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Attorneys for Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization responded on the principle of substantive due process when quizzed on this principle by Thomas.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf I were to ask you what constitutional right protects the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is it privacy? Is it autonomy? What would it be?\u8221\'20\'1D asked Thomas.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s liberty, Your Honor,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Julie Rikelman\uc2 , litigation director for the Center for Reproductive Rights, which represented the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic.\par \par \uc2 Briefs to the court in Dobbs directly challenge that principle, such as the brief from the conservative, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/19/19-1392/185344/20210729162610813_Dobbs%20Amicus%20FINAL%20PDFA.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas Right to Life}}}\uc2 . Attorneys for the group, the conservative legal activists Adam Mortara and Jonathan Mitchell, argued the court does not necessarily need to overturn decisions protecting gay rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut neither should the court hesitate to write an opinion that leaves those decisions hanging by a thread,\u8221\'20\'1D wrote Mortara and Mitchell. \u8220\'20\'1CLawrence and Obergefell, while far less hazardous to human life, are as lawless as Roe.\u8221\'20\'1D The same brief argued women could control their reproduction by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/17/texas-abortion-ban-jonathan-mitchell-supreme-court-brief"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 refraining from sex}}}\uc2 . Neither Mitchell nor Mortara responded to an interview request.\par \par \uc2 Until the supreme court issues a decision, it is unclear exactly how rights protected by substantive due process might be affected. However, scholars consider same-sex and reproductive rights to be the most vulnerable because there is an active political campaign to circumscribe them. By contrast, there is little contemporary criticism of interracial marriage.\par \par \uc2 If Roe v Wade is overruled, \u8220\'20\'1Cit will be on the ground it was a right that was untethered from constitutional text,\u8221\'20\'1D said Murray. If that view prevails on the court, rights to contraception, gay rights and in vitro fertilization could also be quickly implicated, since \u8220\'20\'1Call of these things are untethered from constitutional text and historically were not available in the US\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s more, the history of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights may provide a roadmap for other rights to be hobbled, even if substantive due process prevails.\par \par \uc2 Murray offered the example of a 2017 case, in which three conservative justices, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/16pdf/16-992_868c.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 led by justice Neil Gorsuch}}}\uc2 , argued states could restrict birth certificates of same-sex parents. Gorsuch argued there appeared to be nothing unconstitutional about a \u8220\'20\'1Cbiology-based birth registration regime\u8221\'20\'1D where only one same-sex parent would be listed on the certificate.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is a really good example of how they have dismantled Roe piecemeal and incrementally could be applied to these other rights,\u8221\'20\'1D said Murray.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211211ehcb000rt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
