\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  ruling is even more unsettling than it may seem\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 937 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 December 2021\par 11:33 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In allowing Texas\u8217\'20\'19s outrageous \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to stay in place, the court signaled that it is willing to sacrifice its own legitimacy and power in order to destroy Roe\par \par \uc2 Don\u8217\'20\'19t be fooled by the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s nominal hedging on its endorsement of SB8, the Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that deputizes private citizens to sue anyone who assists in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks\u8217\'20\'19 gestation. In a ruling on Friday, the court held that a lawsuit by Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers could go forward \u8211\'20\'13 but only on narrow grounds. Only those state officials responsible for licensing medical providers may be sued, the court ordered \u8211\'20\'13 no one else involved in the state\u8217\'20\'19s practical maintenance of SB8 is liable. The ruling said, for instance, that the providers could not sue court clerks, those bureaucrats tasked with actually docketing the lawsuits that would enforce SB8.\par \par \uc2 For providers, it seems that the best possible outcome for the suit now is that they may be able to secure an injunction preventing medical providers from being delicensed. These perplexing limits placed by the court on which parties can be sued to challenge SB8 ensures that though the suit against the law will be at least partly allowed to go forward, it will be largely toothless.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, SB8 will remain law. Women in Texas are effectively banned from securing a legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state, even though the still-standing Roe v Wade decision says that they have a right to one. It\u8217\'20\'19s likely that SB8 will remain in effect at least for the duration of Roe\u8217\'20\'19s lifetime \u8211\'20\'13 meaning that Texas women will not be able to obtain legal abortions after six weeks for the foreseeable future. Many of the initial media responses to the court\u8217\'20\'19s opinion emphasized that since the suit was allowed to go forward, on technical grounds, the ruling was a narrow win for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers. But in reality Friday was a massive win for the rightwing Texas government, and for anti-choice forces nationwide.\par \par \uc2 That SB8 has been allowed to take effect \u8211\'20\'13 now for the second time \u8211\'20\'13 by the supreme court reflects the justices\u8217\'20\'19 eagerness to gut \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. The fact of the matter is that the court is already set to overturn Roe and allow states to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  outright. That much was clear to anyone who listened to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/dec/02/supreme-court-signalling-end-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 last week\u8217\'20\'19s oral arguments}}}\uc2  in Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health, a case surrounding the constitutionality of a 15-week ban in Mississippi, which devolved into grim misogynist spectacle as the Republican appointees held court on the supposed ease of giving infants up for adoption and their own robust comfort with overturning long-settled precedent.\par \par \uc2 That ruling is scheduled to come down in late May or early June. When it does, a slim majority of states are expected to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , either immediately or very soon thereafter. That means that soon SB8 \u8211\'20\'13 and the copycat bills that it has inspired in states like Florida and Arkansas \u8211\'20\'13 won\u8217\'20\'19t be necessary for the anti-choice lobby to achieve their aims. Instead of concocting an elaborate enforcement process in which rogue anti-woman vigilantes enforce their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, the states will be able to enforce their bans themselves.\par \par \uc2 SB8, then, and the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s embrace of it, can be understood not only as a harbinger of the justices\u8217\'20\'19 deep contempt for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right, but also of their childish impatience to exert this contempt upon American women. They can\u8217\'20\'19t even wait six months. They want to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right now. In pursuit of this goal, the supreme court has proven itself willing to undermine its own capacity to oversee state laws, to enforce federal supremacy, and to protect constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 The anti-choice substance of the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision in SB8 was not surprising; its embrace of Texas\u8217\'20\'19s tactics perhaps was. Aside from its direct attempt to undermine women\u8217\'20\'19s rights, SB8 also took aim at judicial authority. By banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  long before viability, the law flouted the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s precedents in Roe and Planned Parenthood v Casey. But that much a slew of vehemently anti-choice justices would probably forgive: all six of the Republican appointees clearly believe that Roe was wrongly decided, and at least five of them (all but Roberts, who seems more trepidatious) appear eager to overturn it. But in its novel enforcement mechanism, SB8 sought specifically to evade judicial review \u8211\'20\'13 not just to give the court an opportunity to overturn its own precedent, but to make it so that within Texas borders supreme court precedent didn\u8217\'20\'19t matter.\par \par \uc2 In her dissent, Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  compared SB8 to the views of John C Calhoun \u8211\'20\'13 a nineteenth-century pro-slavery campaigner who argued that states have the right to nullify federal laws that they do not like. America fought its civil war in no small part over this question. By first allowing the SB8 to go into effect, in September, and then by gutting the lawsuit against it this Friday, the supreme court has, shockingly, endorsed a scheme to undermine its own power, and granted a state the ability to evade federal precedent. Nullification, it seems, is back in style.\par \par \uc2 For years, court watchers have wondered whether the justices\u8217\'20\'19 institutionalist instincts would overcome their misogynist ones: if the Court had to choose between maintaining its own power and legitimacy, and overturning Roe, which would it choose? Now, it seems, we have our answer.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211211ehcb001gt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
