\par\par \uc2 Law\par \b \uc2 Supreme court rules Texas  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  providers can sue over ban but won\u8217\'20\'19t stop law\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza, Ed Pilkington, Gloria Oladipo and agencies \par \uc2 976 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 December 2021\par 18:31 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 45\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Justices are allowing the law, the strictest such regulation in America to date, to remain in effect\par \par \uc2 The supreme court ruled on Friday that Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers can sue over the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/01/texas-abortion-law-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 state\u8217\'20\'19s ban}}}\uc2  on most abortions, but the justices are allowing the law, the strictest such regulation in America to date, to remain in effect.\par \par \uc2 The decision is a mixed result for reproductive health advocates at a time when social conservatives seem on the march in America and the supreme court is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/dec/01/oral-arguments-mississippi-abortion-case-begin-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leaning towards}}}\uc2  restricting or outlawing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationally in the future with its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/dec/04/us-supreme-courrt-sonia-sotomayor-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 conservative supermajority}}}\uc2 , engineered by Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 The Texas law is unusual in that it relies on the public, rather than the authorities, to sue those suspected of performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or aiding in the service, although not the patient undergoing the termination. The framing of the law was widely seen as a device to make it harder for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to be challenged in federal court given the lack of state involvement in enforcing the prohibition.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/21pdf/21-463_3ebh.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Friday\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court opinion}}}\uc2 , written by Justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , gives a complicated legal response that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Texas and beyond are likely to receive as a partial but by no means overwhelming victory. In his opinion, Gorsuch makes clear that the court in this case was not addressing the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights per se, or whether the Texas ban was consistent with existing federal law.\par \par \uc2 Those {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/nov/08/catastrophic-implications-un-health-expert-condemns-us-over-threat-to-abortion-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 larger questions}}}\uc2  will fall to a separate case currently before the supreme court, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/nov/30/explainer-mississippi-abortion-rights-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization}}}\uc2 , out of Mississippi, which specifically seeks the overturning of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 , the 1973 landmark supreme court ruling that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/nov/30/abortion-rulings-history-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 paved the way}}}\uc2  for legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide.\par \par \uc2 That case, which could determine the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the US for decades to come, was heard in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/dec/01/five-takeaways-us-supreme-court-mississippi-abortion-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 oral arguments}}}\uc2  last week but is unlikely to be concluded until a ruling is issued next June.\par \par \uc2 The overall result of Friday\u8217\'20\'19s opinion is that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Texas will be allowed to press ahead with their legal challenge to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/01/texas-abortion-law-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 near-total ban on \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 . But their legal path forward has been narrowed and from now on they will be forced to direct their efforts at a small number of state employees.\par \par \uc2 As part of their challenge to SB 8, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics had attempted to sue a Texas state judge. That was dismissed unanimously by the nine justices as being inconsistent with a 1908 supreme court ruling that prohibits federal courts imposing injunctions on state courts.\par \par \uc2 The court ruled by a lesser margin that the providers will be able to continue challenging SB 8 by focusing on four licensing officials who would be involved in taking action against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics under the terms of the new ban. But state court clerks and the attorney general of Texas, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , who had been named as defendants, could not be sued.\par \par \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 , one of the three remaining liberal-leaning justices on the nine-judge supreme court bench, expressed strong views in dissent.\par \par \uc2 She said that by casting their opinion so narrowly, the conservative majority was \u8220\'20\'1Cshrinking\u8221\'20\'1D from its duty to defend the supremacy of the US constitution over the whims of individual states.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe court should have put an end to this madness months ago, before SB 8 went into effect,\u8221\'20\'1D she wrote in a dissenting opinion. \u8220\'20\'1CIt failed to do so then, and it fails again today \u8230\'20\'26 Federal courts can and should issue relief when a state enacts a law that chills the exercise of a constitutional right.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The legal battle to try and stop SB 8 and reopen access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to millions of Texan women will now return to a lower federal court in the state capital, Austin. While it does so, however, the prohibition against almost all abortions in the state will continue to stand.\par \par \uc2 Massachusetts Democratic senator and former presidential candidate Elizabeth Warren said the Friday decision in the Texas case was outrageous.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile SCOTUS has allowed challenges to SB 8 to proceed, it\u8217\'20\'19s outrageous that the Court has again decided not to block Texas\u8217\'20\'19 unconstitutional \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SenWarren/status/1469326405032886273"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tweeted Warren.}}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1CMore Texans are harmed every day this law is allowed to stand. The Senate must pass the Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Protection Act.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The law would codify the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the US into national legislation. The bill passed the House in September and is in limbo in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Also on Friday, the court dismissed an overall challenge to the Texas law from the Biden administration that would have blocked the ban entirely.\par \par \uc2 The law in Texas has been in effect since September, aside from a district court-ordered pause that lasted 48 hours. It bans abortions once cardiac activity is detected, usually around six weeks and before many women know they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 While courts have blocked other state laws effectively banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before a fetus can survive outside the womb, the Texas law has so far avoided a similar fate because it leaves enforcement up to private citizens, a move that many critics have said effectively creates anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bounty hunters.\par \par \uc2 The law allows anyone, anywhere to bring a suit against anyone who helps a woman obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and provides a $10,000 penalty against defendants found to violate the law.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, a judge in Texas had ruled on Thursday that the law violated the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution because it allows private citizens to sue \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers.\par \par \uc2 State district court judge David Peeples was ruling on the law but \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers had already signaled that despite the ruling, they were unlikely to resume the procedure until the supreme court announced its decision.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211210ehca002h3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
