\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Ending Roe v Wade is part of a long campaign to roll back democracy itself\b0\par\par\uc2 Jill Filipovic \par \uc2 984 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 December 2021\par 23:34 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The demise of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights is the outcome of years of Republican work to make it harder for people to vote and stack the bench with rightwing judges\par \par \uc2 American democracy is at the breaking point, and a supreme court ready to gut or overturn Roe v Wade is the latest warning sign. A radical minority is accumulating ever more power, and they\u8217\'20\'19re threatening to undermine equal rights under the law, basic human freedoms, and democracy itself.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the supreme court heard arguments in a case challenging Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s ban on abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy, even for rape and incest survivors. Under the longstanding legal framework of Roe v Wade and Planned Parenthood v Casey, two of the supreme court cases that shape \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the US, states cannot outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the point of fetal viability, when the fetus can survive outside of the woman\u8217\'20\'19s body (states can put restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before that point, so long as those restrictions don\u8217\'20\'19t pose an \u8220\'20\'1Cundue burden\u8221\'20\'1D on women seeking abortions). The Mississippi law violates that longstanding supreme court precedent.\par \par \uc2 Yet the court agreed to hear it anyway, which was the first bad sign \u8211\'20\'13 why hear a case that so clearly flies in the face of what the court has already ruled? Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s oral arguments only contributed to the sense of doom, as a majority of the justices seemed ready and willing to overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 This didn\u8217\'20\'19t happen by accident. The rightwing stranglehold on the courts has been a long-term project achieved by devious means. Republicans blocked \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  from appointing dozens of judges to the federal bench, leaving {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brookings.edu/blog/fixgov/2018/06/04/senate-obstructionism-handed-judicial-vacancies-to-trump/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 those slots}}}\uc2  open for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to fill. He stacked the courts with conservative reactionaries, many of whom were so unqualified that they failed to get the basic endorsement of the \uc2 American Bar Association\uc2  (ABA). Instead of appointing qualified candidates over rightwing stooges, the Trump administration simply {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2017/03/31/us/politics/white-house-american-bar-association-judges.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cut}}}\uc2  the ABA out of the judicial vetting process.\par \par \uc2 The most egregious of these Republican blockades came when Obama tried to appoint \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  to the supreme court seat vacated by \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2 . The right cried foul: it was wrong to change the balance of the court, they said, and it was an election year and therefore unfair to allow Obama a supreme court appointment; voters should decide the next president to pick a supreme court judge.\par \par \uc2 A majority of voters wanted \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  to have that role. But our undemocratic and archaic electoral college rules handed the victory to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the second time in less than two decades that the winner of the majority vote lost the White House.\par \par \uc2 Trump, who ran on a promise of appointing anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  judges who would overturn Roe v Wade, set about doing just that. He appointed \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  to the seat that should have been Garland\u8217\'20\'19s. Then he appointed \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , despite the judge facing credible accusations of sexual assault. Finally, and most insultingly, Trump and his Republican Senate allies rammed through the appointment of the explicitly anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Amy Coney Barrett to the seat vacated by the feminist icon \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 in his last year of office, and despite the supposed rule about a president letting the voters decide before an election.\par \par \uc2 Trump voters \u8211\'20\'13 a minority of Americans in both 2016 and 2020 \u8211\'20\'13 are about to get what they want: an America in which women and girls are forced into pregnancy, childbirth and motherhood; an America in which women are second-class citizens, not entitled to control over the very bodies they live in, forced to risk their lives in the name of \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-life\u8221\'20\'1D misogyny.\par \par \uc2 The rest of us are stuck dealing with these minority religious views imposed on us.\par \par \uc2 Strong majorities of Americans support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and do not want to overturn Roe. And in any case, the supreme court is supposed to be a bulwark against tyranny, an institution that defends and upholds constitutional rights, not one that punts those rights to the states.\par \par \uc2 This court is not that. And that\u8217\'20\'19s because of the shameful rightwing devastation of American democracy. Three members of the conservative supreme court majority, after all, were appointed by a traitorous president who fomented an attempted coup against the United States, and who has continued to undermine the electoral process by claiming that the last election, which he lost fair and square, was stolen. His party has devolved into a cult of personality, so tied to one narcissistic tyrant that it didn\u8217\'20\'19t even bother releasing a political platform in the last presidential election. And because the Republican party knows it will lose if it has to play on an even playing field, its members have been systemically undermining voting rights for years.\par \par \uc2 The demise of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the US is the outcome of years of anti-democratic organizing to make it harder for people to vote, gerrymander districts, pull power from various elected offices when Democrats win them, and stack the bench with rightwing judges who will allow it all to happen.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s terrifying. And of course forcing women into subservience and traditional roles is part of this process \u8211\'20\'13 that\u8217\'20\'19s been the strategy in authoritarian nations throughout history, and it\u8217\'20\'19s a pattern we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing play out now, as the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jill.substack.com/p/american-authoritarianism-hasnt-ended"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 same nations}}}\uc2  that are scaling back democratic norms and processes are also going after women\u8217\'20\'19s rights.\par \par \uc2 That American women are facing a hostile supreme court and are looking at a future without \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights \u8211\'20\'13 and potentially without the constitutional right to contraception \u8211\'20\'13 isn\u8217\'20\'19t a matter of law or \u8220\'20\'1Clife\u8221\'20\'1D. It\u8217\'20\'19s a sign of a democracy in decline.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Jill Filipovic is the author of OK Boomer, Let\u8217\'20\'19s Talk: How My Generation Got Left Behind\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211209ehc8004ef\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
