\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Historical accident\u8217\'20\'19: how  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  came to focus white, evangelical anger\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 1,847 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 December 2021\par 16:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 A short history of the Roe decision\u8217\'20\'19s emergence as a signature cause for the right\par \par \uc2 Public opinion on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US has changed little since 1973, when the supreme court in effect legalized the procedure nationally in its ruling on the case Roe v Wade. According to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/350756/record-high-think-abortion-morally-acceptable.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Gallup}}}\uc2 , which has the longest-running poll on the issue, about four in five Americans believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal, at least in some circumstances.\par \par \uc2 Yet the politics of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have opened deep divisions in the last five decades, which have only grown more profound in recent years of polarization. In 2021, state legislators have passed dozens of restrictions to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, making it the most hostile year to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights on record.\par \par \uc2 This schism played out in the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  on Wednesday, when the new conservative-dominated bench heard oral arguments in the case of Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, the most important \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights case since Roe.\par \par \uc2 In somber arguments, justices questioned whether the state of Mississippi should be allowed to ban nearly all abortions at 15 weeks gestation, nine weeks earlier than the current accepted limit. While the ruling, expected by the end of June next year, is far from a foregone conclusion, justices in the conservative majority appeared to signal their support for severely restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, a right Americans have exercised for two generations.\par \par \uc2 The divisive question among the conservative majority appeared to be whether \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be restricted to earlier than 15 weeks, weakening Roe, or if the precedent set in Roe should be overturned entirely.\par \par \uc2 Summarizing Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s argument, the conservative justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , who was controversially {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2018/oct/06/brett-kavanaugh-confirmed-us-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nominated to the court by Donald Trump in 2018}}}\uc2 , said \u8220\'20\'1Cthe constitution is neither pro-life nor pro-choice \u8230\'20\'26 and leaves the issue to the people to resolve in the democratic process.\u8221\'20\'1D If the issue is returned to the states, 26 states would be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2021/10/26-states-are-certain-or-likely-ban-abortion-without-roe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Ccertain or likely\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  to ban or severely restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 The religious right in the US has been laying the foundations of this decisive challenge to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights for years. According to historians and researchers, it has taken decades of political machinations for the campaign to reach this zenith. The movement has intersected with nearly every major issue in American politics for the last five decades, from segregation to welfare reform to campaign finance.\par \par \uc2 The conservative anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement \u8220\'20\'1Cwas a kind of historical accident\u8221\'20\'1D, said Randall Balmer, a professor of American religious history at Dartmouth University and author of the recently released book {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.eerdmans.com/Products/7934/bad-faith.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bad Faith: Race and the Rise of the Religious Right}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It wasn\u8217\'20\'19t until Republican strategists sought to \u8220\'20\'1Cdeflect attention away from the real narrative\u8221\'20\'1D, which Balmer argues was racial integration, \u8220\'20\'1Cand to advocate on behalf of the fetus\u8221\'20\'1D, that largely apolitical evangelical Christians and Catholics would be united within the Republican party. Balmer argues that advocacy was nascent in 1969.\par \par \uc2 Although the supreme court decision in Brown v Board of Education called for an end to racial segregation in schools in 1954, many schools continued de facto segregation 14 years later.\par \par \uc2 Then, the supreme court weighed in again, and ordered schools to integrate {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.oyez.org/cases/1969/632"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cimmediately\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 . This prompted white southerners to form \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/national/archive/2012/12/in-southern-towns-segregation-academies-are-still-going-strong/266207/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 segregation academies}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D, whites-only private Christian schools which registered as tax-exempt non-profit charities. African American parents in Mississippi sued, arguing this was taxpayer-subsidized discrimination. They won, and in 1971, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/1982/01/12/us/ex-tax-officials-assail-shift-on-school-exemption-status.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tax authorities revoked}}}\uc2  the non-profit status of 111 segregated private schools.\par \par \uc2 In Balmer\u8217\'20\'19s view, revoking the non-profit status of segregated private schools catalyzed evangelical Christian leaders, but even in the early 1970s defense of racial segregation was not a populist message. However, defense of the fetus could be.\par \par \uc2 Republican operations began to test \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a vessel for the collective anxieties of evangelical Christians, and Roe as a shorthand for government intrusion into the family after the sexual revolution of the 1960s. Eventually, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  became the reason for evangelicals to deny the Democratic president Jimmy Carter, himself an evangelical Christian, a second term.\par \par \uc2 Evangelical opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cwasn\u8217\'20\'19t an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement per se\u8221\'20\'1D, said Elmer L Rumminger, an administrator at the then whites-only Christian college Bob Jones University, said in Balmer\u8217\'20\'19s book. \u8220\'20\'1CFor me it was government intrusion into private education.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the anti-feminist Republican activist \uc2 Phyllis Schlafly\uc2  was connecting anxiety about women\u8217\'20\'19s changing roles in society with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In a 1972 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://awpc.cattcenter.iastate.edu/2016/02/02/whats-wrong-with-equal-rights-for-women-1972/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 essay}}}\uc2 , she described the feminist movement as \u8220\'20\'1Canti-family, anti-children, and pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D and the writing of contemporaneous feminists as \u8220\'20\'1Ca series of sharp-tongued, high-pitched whining complaints by unmarried women\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 By the 1978 midterm congressional elections, \uc2 Paul Weyrich\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2008/12/19/us/politics/19weyrich.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one of the architects of modern conservatism}}}\uc2 , was testing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a campaign issue with evangelical Christians with a small fund from the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 . Roman Catholic volunteers distributed hundreds of thousands of leaflets in church parking lots in Iowa, New Hampshire and Minnesota, and their efforts prevailed. Four anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Republicans ousted Democrats.\par \par \uc2 The groundwork laid by Schlafly and Weyrich made \u8220\'20\'1CRoe shorthand for a host of worries about sex equality and sexuality\u8221\'20\'1D, wrote Mary Ziegler, a law professor at Florida State University and author of After Roe: The Lost History of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Debate.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven as late as August 1980, the Reagan-Bush campaign wasn\u8217\'20\'19t certain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would work for them as a political issue,\u8221\'20\'1D said Balmer. However, as Reagan sailed to victory, he was carried in part by religious voters hooked on the promise of a constitutional amendment to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . When a constitutional amendment failed, a new strategy took hold: control the supreme court.\par \par \uc2 Historians said segregation was only one part of a complex and multifaceted movement, which has long seen itself as a human rights campaign. By the 1970s, \u8220\'20\'1Cthere was an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement which was influential and pretty effective in the states that was ready for the new right to work with,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ziegler.\par \par \uc2 In the coming years, Reagan would recast the politics of reproduction through a new racist prism, as he introduced the mythical stereotype of the \u8220\'20\'1Cwelfare queen\u8221\'20\'1D. The image allowed politicians to portray \u8220\'20\'1Call single mothers as persons of color and all persons of color as dependent on public assistance\u8221\'20\'1D, wrote the reproductive rights activists Loretta Ross and Rickie Solinger in their 2017 book Reproductive Justice: An Introduction.\par \par \uc2 The image divorced family wellbeing and welfare support from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and rights. Thus, the \u8220\'20\'1Cbroad middle ground\u8221\'20\'1D of issues that anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and pro-choice voters agreed on became \u8220\'20\'1Cfirmly partisan\u8221\'20\'1D, said Laura Briggs, author of How All Politics Became Reproductive Politics, and professor and chair of women, gender and sexuality studies at University of Massachusetts, Amherst.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 By the 1990s, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists had professionalized. So called \u8220\'20\'1Cright to life\u8221\'20\'1D organizations rallied the base, and religious law firms dedicated themselves to fighting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in courts. The supreme court weighed in on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in 1992, in another watershed case called Planned Parenthood of Southeastern Pennsylvania v Casey. The case allowed states to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as long as such restrictions did not create an \u8220\'20\'1Cundue burden\u8221\'20\'1D on the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and served the purpose of either protecting the woman\u8217\'20\'19s health or unborn life.\par \par \uc2 States hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  passed \u8220\'20\'1CTrap\u8221\'20\'1D laws, or targeted regulations of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, which required \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics to become the \u8220\'20\'1Cfunctional equivalents of hospitals\u8221\'20\'1D, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.yalelawjournal.org/feature/casey-and-the-clinic-closings"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 legal scholars}}}\uc2 . States instituted 24-hour waiting periods for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , state-mandated inaccurate information and invasive sonograms.\par \par \uc2 Many clinics went out of business as they struggled to meet the expensive new requirements, and pregnant people struggled to obtain abortions as they had to travel further and spend more to find a provider.\par \par \uc2 These laws would also play an outsized role in the Dobbs hearing. Conservative justices debated whether they could keep the \u8220\'20\'1Cundue burden\u8221\'20\'1D standard while jettisoning a central tenet of Roe, that women can terminate a pregnancy until a fetus can survive outside the womb, or \u8220\'20\'1Cviability\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhy is 15 weeks not enough time?\u8221\'20\'1D asked Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , a conservative, in the hearings.\par \par \uc2 The politics of reproduction spurred new debates on acceptable restrictions on birth control, stem cell research and sex education during the George W Bush administration. But it was the election of \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , America\u8217\'20\'19s first Black president, that supercharged Republican opposition.\par \par \uc2 In 2010, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2010/sep/15/tea-party-republican-primary-victories"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tea Party swept the midterm}}}\uc2  elections. More extreme candidates entered Congress and statehouses through the practice of challenging incumbents in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2019/jun/18/us-anti-abortion-bans-backlash-gerrymandering"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 districts gerrymandered}}}\uc2  to be reliably Republican. And, in a decision not typically thought of as an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  victory, the chief counsel for National Right to Life successfully argued a supreme court case that would unleash vast sums of dark money into American elections \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Citizens United\uc2  v \uc2 Federal Election Commission\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, over time with other conservative allies, worked to change things like the rules of campaign finance for the conservative movement,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ziegler. \u8220\'20\'1CAnti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawyers played an integral part in cases like \uc2 Citizens United\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 By the time \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ran for president, evangelical Protestants had become more anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than the Catholic voters who were once the bedrock of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocacy. Seventy-seven per cent of white evangelical Christians say the procedure should be illegal, compared with just 43% of Catholics, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/fact-tank/2021/06/17/key-facts-about-the-abortion-debate-in-america/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pew Research Center}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Trump harnessed the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/nov/06/white-evangelical-christians-supported-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 anger of white evangelicals}}}\uc2  for a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2016/jul/13/evangelical-christians-donald-trump-support-increasing-poll"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 victory in 2016}}}\uc2 , with a mix of hardline anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politicsand xenophobic nativism. Trump abandoned his 1999 stance as \u8220\'20\'1Cvery {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/meet-the-press/video/trump-in-1999-i-am-very-pro-choice-480297539914"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pro-choice}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D, saying there should be \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2016/mar/30/donald-trump-women-abortions-punishment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 punishment}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D for women who have abortions, and promised to nominate conservative supreme court justices who would \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2018/jul/08/trump-supreme-court-nomination-battle-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 automatically}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D overturn Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 Today, overwhelmingly white \u8220\'20\'1CChristian nationalist\u8221\'20\'1D voters believe their religion should be privileged in public life, a goal to be attained \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://religionnews.com/2020/06/02/with-bibles-and-flash-grenades-trump-walks-the-christian-nationalist-walk/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 by any means necessary}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D, according to social researchers such as Indiana University associate professor Andrew Whitehead.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Supreme court decisions are notoriously difficult to predict, but \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists believe Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s hearing shows that conservative justices are ready to significantly weaken or perhaps overturn Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 If that happens, young, poor people of color will disproportionately suffer, forced to carry unwanted pregnancies to term. Such an outcome is so severe {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/nov/08/catastrophic-implications-un-health-expert-condemns-us-over-threat-to-abortion-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 human rights advocates}}}\uc2  have said state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans would violate United Nations conventions against torture and place the US in the company of a shrinking number of countries with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the court\u8217\'20\'19s three outnumbered liberal justices argued neither the science, the enormous consequences of pregnancy nor the American polity had changed since the court last decided a watershed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights case. But, because of the work of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politicians, the makeup of the court\u8217\'20\'19s bench had.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWill this institution survive the stench that this creates in the public perception that the constitution and its reading are just political acts?\u8221\'20\'1D asked the liberal justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t see how it is possible.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 This article was amended on 6 December 2021 to correct a name\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211205ehc5000gr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
