\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 The \u8216\'20\'18stench\u8217\'20\'19 of politicization: \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s supreme court warning\b0\par\par\uc2 Ed Pilkington \par \uc2 1,232 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 December 2021\par 07:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Oral arguments over the Mississippi \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case this week showed the threat to Roe v Wade from an increasingly politicized court\par \par \uc2 About 11 minutes into this week\u8217\'20\'19s hearing on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights at the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 , the floor was taken by \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 , one of the three beleaguered liberal-leaning justices left on the court after its sharp rightward shift under {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2021/may/23/us-supreme-court-trump-judicial-appointments"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Sotomayor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2021/12/01/politics/read-transcript-dobbs-jackson-womens-health/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 began}}}\uc2  by noting that in the past 30 years no fewer than 15 justices of all political backgrounds had supported the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to the point of fetal viability. Only four had objected.\par \par \uc2 Now after so many years of relative consensus, the legality of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  enshrined in the landmark 1973 ruling Roe v Wade and reaffirmed in 1992 in Planned Parenthood v Casey was suddenly on the line.\par \par \uc2 Politicians in Mississippi, Sotomayor remarked (while leaving it unsaid that they were rightwing Republicans), had devised new legislation to ban abortions after just 15 weeks of pregnancy. By these politicians\u8217\'20\'19 own admission, their bills were targeted specifically at the three new justices on the supreme court (all appointed by Trump, though she left that unspoken too).\par \par \uc2 Then she went in for the kill.\par \par \uc2 She addressed the danger posed by the court\u8217\'20\'19s sudden and apparently politically motivated change of heart not just to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights but to the rule of law itself.\par \par \uc2 If the nation\u8217\'20\'19s highest court, with its newly constituted Trumpian majority, were to go along with the ploy set for it by Mississippi and throw out half a century of settled law affirming a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose, then what would happen to the court\u8217\'20\'19s legitimacy as a place in American democracy that rises above the cut and thrust of grubby partisanship?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWill this institution survive the stench that this creates in the public perception that the constitution and its reading are just political acts?\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t see how it is possible.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Stench. The word ricocheted off the august walls of the courtroom like a bullet.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was a shocking moment,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Sifill_LDF/status/1466082967294562316"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2 Sherrilyn Ifill\uc2 , director-counsel of the NAACP Legal Defense Fund. \u8220\'20\'1CAn unadorned recognition of the legitimacy issues that are clearly preoccupying a number of the justices.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For Stephen Vladeck, a professor of constitutional law at the University of Texas at Austin, the takeaway of this week\u8217\'20\'19s hearing was not how many justices were preoccupied with the reputational damage facing an increasingly politicised court, but how few. \u8220\'20\'1CTo me, the single most distressing feature of Justice Sotomayor\u8217\'20\'19s arguments was how little anyone else seemed to care,\u8221\'20\'1D he told \uc2 the Guardian\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Vladeck said he was dismayed by the \u8220\'20\'1Ccasualness with which so many of the justices seemed to be taking an issue that is so central to so many women. A ruling that gets rid of Roe would be enormously damaging in the eyes of millions of Americans, yet some of the conservative justices don\u8217\'20\'19t seem to think that\u8217\'20\'19s important.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The perception of nonchalance towards the integrity of the court among the six conservative justices now in the majority is striking. In advance of last week\u8217\'20\'19s supercharged hearing, several of those same justices bent over backwards to try to convince the American people that they are neutral servants of the constitution.\par \par \uc2 The three justices appointed by Trump have been especially keen to portray themselves as having not a partisan bone in their body. \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , Trump\u8217\'20\'19s first of the three appointments, insisted in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://money.yahoo.com/gorsuch-says-us-supreme-court-220536719.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 2019}}}\uc2  that it was \u8220\'20\'1Crubbish\u8221\'20\'1D to imply that the justices were \u8220\'20\'1Clike politicians with robes\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 More recently Amy Coney Barrett, another of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s triumvirate of appointees, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.courier-journal.com/story/news/politics/mitch-mcconnell/2021/09/12/justice-amy-coney-barrett-supreme-court-decisions-arent-political/8310849002/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told an audience}}}\uc2  in Kentucky that the supreme court was not \u8220\'20\'1Ccomprised of a bunch of partisan hacks\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But she was speaking at the McConnell Center at the \uc2 University of Louisville\uc2  and was introduced at the event by the politician after whom the venue is named \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the top Republican in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 . It was his shenanigans, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2016/mar/16/obama-nominates-merrick-garland-supreme-court-dc-appeals-court-judge"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blocking}}}\uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s confirmation to the court in 2016 on grounds that it was in an election year then {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2020/oct/25/mitch-mcconnell-amy-coney-barrett-supreme-court-republicans-democrats-mike-pence-covid"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rushing through}}}\uc2  Barrett\u8217\'20\'19s confirmation much closer to election day in 2020, that gave Trump his three picks.\par \par \uc2 But it is the third of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court proteges, \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , whose position is perhaps most glaring. During his confirmation process in 2018 Kavanaugh went to great lengths to underline his respect for the decisions made by his predecessors on the court, and for the legal doctrine known as stare decisis, which requires justices to honor past rulings in all but exceptional cases.\par \par \uc2 Kavanaugh {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/d843c7076e2b44fbbec1a4d26ef9d830/Kavanaugh-calls-Roe-V.-Wade-settled,-but-Dem-critics-unmoved"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 assured senators}}}\uc2  worried about his stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that he saw Roe v Wade as \u8220\'20\'1Csettled law\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He went even further in his conversations with \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 , the relatively moderate Republican senator from Maine on whose vote Kavanaugh depended. When she {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.collins.senate.gov/newsroom/senator-collins-announces-she-will-vote-confirm-judge-kavanaugh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced}}}\uc2  her decision to back him for the supreme court, she revealed what he had said to her during private conversations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere has been considerable \u8230\'20\'26 concern that Judge Kavanaugh would seek to overturn Roe v Wade,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CProtecting this right is important to me. As Judge Kavanaugh asserted to me, a long-established precedent is not something to be trimmed, narrowed, discarded or overlooked.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But when it came round to Kavanaugh\u8217\'20\'19s turn to speak in this week\u8217\'20\'19s debate he read out a long list of supreme court cases in which prior precedents had been overturned. He left observers with the clear impression that he was preparing to do precisely what he promised Collins and her fellow senators that he would not do \u8211\'20\'13 run roughshod over a pillar of constitutional law.\par \par \uc2 The pointed interventions of the Trump justices and their conservative peers in this week\u8217\'20\'19s hearing have led most observers convinced that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the US are likely to be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/dec/01/oral-arguments-mississippi-abortion-case-begin-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 grossly restricted or abolished outright}}}\uc2  when the court rules next June. That would be uncannily as Trump himself had predicted.\par \par \uc2 In a televised debate during the 2016 presidential race, Trump was asked by the Fox News host Chris Wallace whether he wanted the court, including any justices he might appoint as president, to overturn the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He replied: \u8220\'20\'1CI am pro-life, and I will be appointing pro-life judges. I would think that that will go back to the individual states.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trump did go on to appoint anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  judges, and they are now poised to send control back to individual states, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/nov/29/us-abortion-supreme-court-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 21 of which currently have laws in place}}}\uc2  that would effectively ban abortions overnight were Roe v Wade overturned.\par \par \uc2 Vladeck fears that the vast and growing disconnect between what the conservative justices say they are doing \u8211\'20\'13 impartially and faithfully upholding the law of the land, and what they are actually doing \u8211\'20\'13 playing along with the machinations of politicians in states like Mississippi, bodes very ill for the legitimacy of the court.\par \par \uc2 In the long run it could also harm America\u8217\'20\'19s future as a country of laws.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPublic perception matters,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe more the court appears to be guided by contemporary partisan preferences as opposed to permanent legal principles, the harder it will be for millions of Americans on the wrong side of these cases to understand why they should be bound by them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211204ehc4000gq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
