\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 America\u8217\'20\'19s deep political divisions play out on courthouse steps as justices hear  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  case\b0\par\par\uc2 David Smith in Washington \par \uc2 1,004 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 December 2021\par 18:04 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights acknowledge that in many states, restrictions have already ushered in a \u8216\'20\'18post-Roe world\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Hannah Wolfe, 24, brandished a rubber model that she said depicted a 16-week-old foetus. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s important for people to see the humanity of a child in the womb,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CYou can see the fingers, you can see the toes, you can detect a heartbeat at this stage.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Wolfe was among the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protesters who turned out in force at the supreme court on Wednesday with a sense that victory is finally within reach. In contrast to the sombre arguments being heard in the ornate, marble-clad court, in the streets below the air filled with a cacophony of speeches, shouts, chants and songs from activists on both sides.\par \par \uc2 Wolfe and fellow members of Students for Life of America held a banner that said, \u8220\'20\'1CReverse Roe now,\u8221\'20\'1D urging the highest court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative majority to defy public opinion polls by overturning Roe v Wade, the landmark 1973 decision that enshrined a woman\u8217\'20\'19s constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is such a historic moment for the pro-life movement and the cause of a post-Roe America,\u8221\'20\'1D said Wolfe, who is from Arlington, Virginia. \u8220\'20\'1CThe reality is that women do not need abortions. Our goal is that they should have the resources to be supported every step of the way.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is the most exciting opportunity we\u8217\'20\'19ve had in my lifetime. It\u8217\'20\'19s going to be the first nail in the coffin of Roe v Wade.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As the justices heard a case that involves a Mississippi law that seeks to ban most abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy, America\u8217\'20\'19s deep political divisions could be seen playing out in noise and colour on the stage of the nearby plaza, watched by TV cameras and \uc2 Capitol police\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Crowds had begun to gather before dawn. Reproductive rights defenders and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers carried signs that said, \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is healthcare,\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1CProtect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access,\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1CVote out anti-choice politicians,\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1CStop the bans,\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1CMy body my choice\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1CHands off Roe!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 About {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/fact-tank/2021/05/06/about-six-in-ten-americans-say-abortion-should-be-legal-in-all-or-most-cases/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 six in 10 Americans}}}\uc2  believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases. But on this occasion, pro-choice protesters appeared to be outnumbered by the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lobby, which has spent decades on legal strategising to build towards this moment. Their placards included: \u8220\'20\'1CThe future is anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1CProtect me: I\u8217\'20\'19m 15 weeks old,\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1CWomen deserve better than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1CNo state needs to wait: ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  now!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 They went on: \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is violence,\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1CStop calling violence feminism,\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1CWomen\u8217\'20\'19s rights begin in the womb,\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1CLove Life,\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1CLife is worth it,\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1CAmerica, time for you to bless God,\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1CGod hates hands that shed innocent blood.\u8221\'20\'1D Other signs read \u8220\'20\'1CChrist is Lord, not the courts, defy them,\u8221\'20\'1D \u8220\'20\'1CQueer atheist pro-life\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1CSupreme court: overturn Roe v Wade!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 There were gruesome and graphic images that claimed to show aborted babies and to suggest that science is now on their side of the debate. One sign drew comparison with the civil rights movement of the 1960s. Another used a slogan familiar from Donald Trump campaign rallies: \u8220\'20\'1CDon\u8217\'20\'19t tread on me.\u8221\'20\'1D Another said simply: \u8220\'20\'1CPray.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 About 20 students from the evangelical \uc2 Liberty University\uc2  gathered in a circle, holding hands and heads bowed in silent prayer. Elsewhere in the crowd, a band of Roe opponents played music and sang. Many people were born long after the 1973 ruling, one of the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s most longstanding precedents.\par \par \uc2 Ethan Strohmetz, a 19-year-old student, said: \u8220\'20\'1CHopefully Roe v Wade will be overturned. It legalised the killing of unborn human beings. I\u8217\'20\'19m very confident the pro-life side is going to win this case. It\u8217\'20\'19s not about women\u8217\'20\'19s rights; it\u8217\'20\'19s about human rights.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Advocates for reproductive rights acknowledged the sizable and vocal show by their opponents. Tamara Marzouk, director of youth \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access at Advocates for Youth, said: \u8220\'20\'1CNo matter the turnout, we\u8217\'20\'19re going to continue turning out to support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. It\u8217\'20\'19s disheartening but it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean we lose hope.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 If Roe v Wade were to be overturned, 26 states are poised to restrict or ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, possibly forcing people to travel huge distances to more liberal states for the procedure. Young people would be disproportionately affected, Marzouk warned, since they often have less money, less ability to travel and less ability to take time off work.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNo matter what happens, young people are not going to let the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access disappear. Young people are sharing information with each other about self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with medication. There is a common misconception it would be dangerous but it is extremely safe and effective up to 12 weeks of pregnancy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Some activists put Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s hearing in context by noting that conservative states have been chipping away at reproductive rights for years. Jeryl Hayes, movement building director of If/When/How: Lawyering for Reproductive Justice, said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe know people are already living in a post-Roe world in parts of our country.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis has been a long time coming. We anticipated that a case like this would reach the supreme court but we know the restrictions have been building state by state.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It was a difficult day for women such as Jan Greenberg, 74, a retired nurse who had an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when she was 19. Such underground procedures were often risky. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was very frightening but I made it,\u8221\'20\'1D she recalled, holding a \u8220\'20\'1CJews for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  justice\u8221\'20\'1D sign.\par \par \uc2 Roe v Wade, a ruling handed down almost 50 years ago, had felt like a turning point, Greenberg added.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt made things so much easier. But having lived through that time to now, I\u8217\'20\'19m not surprised. The right has been working on it ever since Roe v Wade. Who knows what will happen? It\u8217\'20\'19s a little scary.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211201ehc1002s6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
