\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Conservative US supreme court justices signal support for restricting  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  in pivotal case\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 1,147 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 December 2021\par 18:05 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 26\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Case poses a direct threat to the legal underpinnings of the landmark 1973 Roe v Wade ruling that guaranteed the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Conservative justices in the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  have signaled their support for curbing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access during oral arguments in the most important reproductive rights case in decades, threatening the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access across the country.\par \par \uc2 Campaigners have warned the case poses a direct threat to the legal underpinnings of Roe v Wade, a landmark 1973 decision that guaranteed the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In their lines of questioning on Wednesday, liberal justices warned against abandoning important legal precedent, while conservatives argued for reviewing it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe constitution is neither pro-life nor pro-choice \u8230\'20\'26 and leaves the issue to the people to resolve in the democratic process,\u8221\'20\'1D said the conservative justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , who was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2018/oct/06/brett-kavanaugh-confirmed-us-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 controversially appointed}}}\uc2  to the bench by former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The case is named for \uc2 Dr Thomas Dobbs\uc2 , the head of Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s health department and the official seen as the chief enforcer of the law, and Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, the last \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in Mississippi. The court is expected to issue a decision on the case in June 2022, but oral arguments offer clues to the justices\u8217\'20\'19 thinking ahead of the ruling.\par \par \uc2 The case centers on whether the state of Mississippi can ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 15 weeks\u8217\'20\'19 gestation, roughly nine weeks before bans are permitted under current law.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of protesters gathered outside the court to show support for, or opposition to, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights \u8211\'20\'13 one of the most contentious issues in the US. Some held signs that read \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is healthcare\u8221\'20\'1D, while \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents held signs reading \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is murder\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Crepent or perish\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Dobbs is the first \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights case to be heard by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2021/oct/11/us-supreme-court-john-roberts-politics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new, conservative-dominated bench}}}\uc2 . Trump successfully confirmed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/oct/25/trump-judges-supreme-court-justices-judiciary"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 three justices to the court}}}\uc2 , and thus created a new conservative supermajority with six of nine justices leaning to the right. While far from a foregone conclusion, questions from conservative justices appeared to show there was not a majority to uphold Roe v Wade in its current form.\par \par \uc2 Conservative justices including Kavanaugh indicated an interest in overturning Roe, and allowing states to determine their own laws. In that scenario, more than half of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2021/10/26-states-are-certain-or-likely-ban-abortion-without-roe-heres-which-ones-and-why"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US states}}}\uc2  are expected to ban or severely restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 But they also considered upholding Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, which would not overturn Roe but would substantially weaken it.\par \par \uc2 Liberal justices challenged Scott Stewart, the solicitor general of Mississippi, on whether the state\u8217\'20\'19s argument was fundamentally religious; whether overruling Roe would lead to other rights being overturned, such as gay marriage and the right to contraception; and how the court\u8217\'20\'19s legitimacy would be affected by potentially overturning such an important decision.\par \par \uc2 Referring to the 1992 supreme court ruling Planned Parenthood of Southeastern Pennsylvania v Casey, which reaffirmed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and prohibited laws imposing an \u8220\'20\'1Cundue burden\u8221\'20\'1D on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, the liberal justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  said the court should not bow to political pressure in overturning Roe. He said such a ruling would \u8220\'20\'1Csubvert the court\u8217\'20\'19s legitimacy\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps the harshest questioning came from Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 , who asked how the court could overturn a \u8220\'20\'1Cwatershed\u8221\'20\'1D case such as Roe with its reputation intact.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe right of a woman to choose, the right to control her own body, has been clearly set since Casey and never challenged. You want us to reject that line of viability and adopt something different,\u8221\'20\'1D Sotomayor said.\par \par \uc2 Sotomayor said Mississippi brought its new challenge purely because of changes on the supreme court bench.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWill this institution survive the stench this creates?\u8221\'20\'1D Sotomayor asked, saying it would give the impression that the constitution and its interpretation is based purely on politics. \u8220\'20\'1CIf people think it is all political \u8230\'20\'26 how will the court survive?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Conservative justices adopted different lines of questioning, including whether the constitution is \u8220\'20\'1Cneutral\u8221\'20\'1D on the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; whether there was a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \u8220\'20\'1Chistory and tradition\u8221\'20\'1D of the US; whether viability was an \u8220\'20\'1Carbitrary\u8221\'20\'1D line; and whether a case can be overruled because it was \u8220\'20\'1Cegregiously wrong\u8221\'20\'1D at the time it was handed down.\par \par \uc2 Kavanaugh questioned \uc2 Julie Rikelman\uc2  , an attorney for the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2  who represents the Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, about whether the court should be involved in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights at all. He asked her to respond to Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s argument that, as he put it, \u8220\'20\'1Cthe court has been forced \u8230\'20\'26 to pick sides on the most contentious social debate in American life.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Rikelman said that the court had already heard and rejected those arguments and abandoning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a constitutional right would not be neutral: \u8220\'20\'1CThe constitution provides a guarantee of liberty.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The conservative justice Amy Coney Barrett, also appointed under Trump, asked whether women could simply \u8220\'20\'1Cterminate their parental rights\u8221\'20\'1D at the end of a pregnancy by giving a child up for adoption, instead of seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is, without question, an infringement on bodily autonomy which we have another contexts like vaccines,\u8221\'20\'1D said Coney Barrett. \u8220\'20\'1CBut it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t seem to me to follow that pregnancy and parenthood are all part of the same burden.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , also conservative, appeared to express interest in upholding Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s 15-week ban. \u8220\'20\'1CViability,\u8221\'20\'1D generally regarded as 24 weeks, \u8220\'20\'1Cdoesn\u8217\'20\'19t have anything to do with choice,\u8221\'20\'1D said Roberts. \u8220\'20\'1CWhy is 15 weeks not enough time?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates had considered the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to hear the case to be a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/may/18/us-supreme-court-abortion-test-case-mississippi-precedent"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 warning sign}}}\uc2 . The court could have cited the precedent set by Roe to refuse to hear the case, but the fact it was accepted for oral arguments meant at least four justices saw merit in revisiting Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 A ban on nearly all abortions at or after 15 weeks would represent a huge blow for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access across the county. To allow such a restriction, the court would need to issue a ruling that would overturn or substantially weaken Roe v Wade, which in many states is the only legal protection guaranteeing the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 If Roe v Wade were overturned, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2021/10/26-states-are-certain-or-likely-ban-abortion-without-roe-heres-which-ones-and-why"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 26 states are }}}\uc2  expected to move to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  illegal. About 40 million women of reproductive age, or about 58% of the people who can get pregnant in the US, live in states considered hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Law enforcement experts believe criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , again, would lead to a public health crisis and a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/nov/29/us-abortion-supreme-court-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wave of prosecutions}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The case comes after conservative justices {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/04/texas-abortion-ruling-reaction"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 allowed Texas to undermine Roe}}}\uc2  within its borders. As a result, Texas\u8217\'20\'19s law has effectively ended access to the vast majority of abortions in America\u8217\'20\'19s second largest state geographically, where more than six million women of reproductive age reside.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211201ehc1000un\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
