\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The supreme court is signalling that it\u8217\'20\'19s ready to end Roe v Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 1,219 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 December 2021\par 11:17 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Predictions that the court would keep \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a constitutional right are starting to look incredibly optimistic\par \par \uc2 It went worse than had been expected, and expectations were already low. As the supreme court prepared to hear oral arguments in Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, a lawsuit over a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in Mississippi that constitutes the most serious challenge to Roe v Wade in a generation, many court watchers predicted a massive rollback of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. But the line among reasonable pundits was that the court, fearing censure from a largely pro-choice American public, would attempt to have its cake and eat it too \u8211\'20\'13 allowing states to impose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans earlier in pregnancy, but keeping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a constitutional right intact.\par \par \uc2 The most convincing version of this argument came from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://slate.com/news-and-politics/2021/11/dobbs-abortion-roe-casey-supreme-court.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Slate\u8217\'20\'19s Mark Joseph Stern}}}\uc2 , who predicted that the court, like it did in 1992\u8217\'20\'19s Planned Parenthood v Casey, might weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right without abandoning it entirely. In Casey, the supreme court lessened the standard of scrutiny applied to state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions \u8211\'20\'13 from a robust \u8220\'20\'1Cstrict scrutiny\u8221\'20\'1D standard to a more malleable \u8220\'20\'1Cundue burden\u8221\'20\'1D standard \u8211\'20\'13 and affirmed that states could ban abortions outright after fetal viability, the point of gestation at which a fetus can survive outside the womb, usually at about 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Stern, like many others, predicted that the court might impose an even more deferential legal test on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1Crational basis review\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 and eliminate the viability standard. The result would be that states could ban and restrict abortions more easily, even before viability, but they would still not be allowed to ban abortions entirely. \u8220\'20\'1CThe court could move back the point at which states can prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  outright from 24 weeks to 15 or perhaps 12, the end of the first trimester,\u8221\'20\'1D Stern wrote. \u8220\'20\'1CA diminished right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would survive, battered but extant.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 And yet the end of the viability standard would still have been practically disastrous for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access on the ground, as well as for women\u8217\'20\'19s freedom and dignity. This much was elegantly explained by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nymag.com/intelligencer/2021/11/abortion-fetal-viability-line.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York\u8217\'20\'19s Irin Carmon}}}\uc2 , who wrote that attacks from conservatives over the past 30 years have increased the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right\u8217\'20\'19s legal reliance on the viability standard, even as developments in pre- and neo-natal care have pushed viability itself earlier in pregnancy. \u8220\'20\'1CIf a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 15 weeks is allowed for whatever reason, why not draw the line at six?\u8221\'20\'1D Carmon asked.\par \par \uc2 The best-case scenario was legal chaos, misogynist lawmaking, and a diminished right to bodily autonomy for women\par \par \uc2 Getting rid of the viability standard, but still leaving the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  technically intact, would in practice invite an anarchic scramble, as conservative states rushed to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as early as possible and push the limit back sooner and sooner in pregnancy. Julie Rickelman, a longtime \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocate and the lawyer representing Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s lone \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in the Dobbs case, but it bluntly: If viability goes, Roe is effectively no longer good law. \u8220\'20\'1CIf the court upholds this law, it will be discarding the viability line and overruling Roe,\u8221\'20\'1D she told Carmon. \u8220\'20\'1CThat is the key line in the law that has protected people\u8217\'20\'19s access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In other words, the best-case scenario was legal chaos, misogynist lawmaking, a diminished right to bodily autonomy for women, and millions more people subject to forced pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 But even these predictions \u8211\'20\'13 which pass for \u8220\'20\'1Coptimism\u8221\'20\'1D among legal observers now that the supreme court is held in the chokehold of a conservative supermajority \u8211\'20\'13 proved too rosy. At oral arguments in Dobbs on Wednesday, five of the court\u8217\'20\'19s six conservatives showed little interest in maintaining Roe while getting rid of viability. Instead, they were focused on eliminating Roe, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right, entirely. By the end, it seemed likely that conservatives have a crucial five votes to rule that the constitution does not protect the right to end a pregnancy: \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and Amy Coney Barrett.\par \par \uc2 The lone exception among the conservatives was Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , who seemed almost desperate to direct attention to the viability line. Over the course of arguments, Roberts repeatedly returned to the viability question, emphasizing that it was rejected as a possible standard in the initial 1973 Roe decision and only adopted later, in Casey. But none of the other conservatives took the bait.\par \par \uc2 The two \u8220\'20\'1Cswing\u8221\'20\'1D votes \u8211\'20\'13 if such an extremely and committedly conservative court can be said to have such a thing \u8211\'20\'13 are Amy Coney Barrett and \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 . In a display of the impotence that has marked his career as chief justice, Roberts was unable to persuade either of them to take up his apparently preferred proposition of maintaining a shell of Roe while gutting the viability standard.\par \par \uc2 Barrett suggests if a woman is pregnant and does not want to be, she can gestate and birth a child, and then simply give it away\par \par \uc2 Instead, Kavanaugh spent much of his speaking time assuring his colleagues that they need not be bound by Roe\u8217\'20\'19s precedent, listing a long litany of cases in which the supreme court reversed its own prior decisions. Barrett, meanwhile, emphasized the availability of adoption as a supposedly adequate alternative to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at one point asserting that so-called \u8220\'20\'1Csafe haven\u8221\'20\'1D laws, which allow birth mothers to surrender their parental rights and leave their infants in the care of others without punishment immediately after they give birth, offer an adequate remedy for pregnant women who cannot or do not wish to become parents. The idea was that if a woman is pregnant and does not want to be, an acceptable outcome would be for her to gestate and birth a child, and then simply give it away.\par \par \uc2 The hardest-line conservatives, meanwhile, offered even more grim and ominous assessments of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a matter of law, and their sadistic and extremist views give some indication of where the court may be heading in future cases. Both Alito and Thomas referred repeatedly to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as \u8220\'20\'1Ctaking a life\u8221\'20\'1D, and indicated that they would be open to recognizing fetal personhood. Until now, post-viability \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans have rested on the legal idea that the state has an interest in protecting fetal life that overrides a woman\u8217\'20\'19s interest in controlling her own body after that point. But Alito and Thomas suggested that they think that interest belongs not only to the state, but to the fetus itself, and that this interest begins very early. \u8220\'20\'1CThe fetus has an interest in having a life,\u8221\'20\'1D Alito said at one point. \u8220\'20\'1CThat doesn\u8217\'20\'19t change from the point before viability and after viability.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The suggestion that a fetus might have interests in its own right \u8211\'20\'13 interests that can be seen as equal or greater than the interests of the woman carrying it \u8211\'20\'13 is a dramatic step in anti-choice jurisprudence, one with dramatic implications for women\u8217\'20\'19s healthcare, freedoms, and access to public life. After Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s oral arguments, it seems certain that Roe v Wade will soon be overturned. For this court, that\u8217\'20\'19s just the beginning.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211202ehc2001uq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
