\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 A \u8216\'20\'18fundamental\u8217\'20\'19 right: a timeline of US  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights since Roe v Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 2,400 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 December 2021\par 14:59 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 When the all-male supreme court ruled to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in 1973, activists didn\u8217\'20\'19t foresee the fight that lay ahead\par \par \uc2 The landmark 1973 supreme court case Roe v Wade guaranteed the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Now, nearly five decades later, that right is under threat as the court prepares to hear oral arguments in a new case, Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization.\par \par \uc2 How could a decision that was not met with widespread protest at the time be under threat less than half a century later?\par \par \uc2 Over the years, a vocal minority of socially conservative Americans have steadily gained political power, passed many state-level challenges to the decision and, in some extreme cases, resorted to violence and intimidation in an attempt to reduce access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Although six in 10 adults in the US say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/fact-tank/2021/06/17/key-facts-about-the-abortion-debate-in-america/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 all or most}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D circumstances, Congress has never enshrined the right to terminate a pregnancy in statute. That has left \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access vulnerable during a period of sustained legal attacks during the Trump administration and led to the precarious position of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the US today.\par \par \uc2 Here are some of the key moments in the almost 50 years that have passed since the Roe v Wade ruling.\par \par \uc2 1973\par \par \uc2 In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.oyez.org/cases/1971/70-18"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 7-2 decision}}}\uc2 , the all-male supreme court ruled that the constitution protects the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Justices found \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a \u8220\'20\'1Cfundamental\u8221\'20\'1D right for a woman\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Clife and future\u8221\'20\'1D, and that Texas had violated the rights of \u8220\'20\'1CJane Roe\u8221\'20\'1D when an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban prevented her from obtaining one.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen the decision came down, we were elated,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Eleanor Smeal\uc2 , who was active in the feminist movement at the time and who is now president of the Feminist Majority Foundation. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was a celebration, it was happiness, and people thought the fight was over \u8211\'20\'13 we won.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe movement wasn\u8217\'20\'19t thinking of, \u8216\'20\'18Oh my God, we\u8217\'20\'19re going to have to fight to protect this,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Smeal added. \u8220\'20\'1CWe couldn\u8217\'20\'19t imagine anyone would hammer away.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The decision was preceded by decades of activism, with momentum decidedly on the side of those working to liberalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws. Constituencies as diverse as radical feminists, clergy and doctors came together to call for reform to criminal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere was a big movement for legalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the supreme court decision. I think people forget that,\u8221\'20\'1D Smeal said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe talk about back-alley \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but [women] were dying\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 People, especially those on low incomes, gained access to safe and legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to the point a fetus could survive outside the womb. Big-city hospitals shuttered {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2003-jan-21-me-patt21-story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cseptic \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 \u8221\'20\'1D wards}}}\uc2 , where many poor people had died, infected and injured, from desperate attempts to end pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 People also gained access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through Medicaid, a public health insurance program for low-income people and those living with disabilities. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  joined contraceptives, legalized for unmarried people just one year earlier, as a newly available method of family planning.\par \par \uc2 The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement as we know it today had yet to coalesce, but the stridently anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and anti-LGBTQ+ North Carolina US senator Jesse Helms did successfully pass the Helms amendment, which banned the use of foreign health aid to promote \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a method of family planning\u8221\'20\'1D overseas. The amendment remains in effect.\par \par \uc2 1976\par \par \uc2 The Hyde amendment, a temporary budget rider that barred Medicaid from paying for abortions, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://repository.law.umich.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=3614&context=mlr"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed}}}\uc2  in the House of Representatives with a large majority. It was encouraged by Catholic bishops, and, unlike in today\u8217\'20\'19s polarized environment, votes were not split strictly along party lines. More than 100 Democrats supported the measure, and 32 Republicans opposed it.\par \par \uc2 At the time, some representatives argued the Hyde amendment unfairly discriminated against poor people. The law, still in place, has made it far more difficult for low-income people to obtain abortions, often forcing them to pay hundreds of dollars for the procedure. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/gpr/2003/03/lessons-roe-will-past-be-prologue"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Three-quarters}}}\uc2  of people who seek abortions in the US are low-income. The policy disproportionately affects people of color.\par \par \uc2 This period would represent a turning point in Republican politics. Republican political activists such as \uc2 Phyllis Schlafly\uc2  worked to bring together evangelicals and conservative Catholics by uniting them in concern about women\u8217\'20\'19s changing place in society and how they would exercise new rights, such as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Until then, legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/otm/segments/when-republicans-wanted-abortion-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 supported by many Republicans}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSchlafly and her supporters argue that 1960s liberalism celebrates the individual and promotes the rights of the individual at the expense of the family and at the expense of a kind of moral order,\u8221\'20\'1D the Harvard historian Jill Lepore told {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/otm/segments/when-republicans-wanted-abortion-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 On the Media}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  becomes a linchpin\u8221\'20\'1D for this argument, she added.\par \par \uc2 The same year, the IRS revoked the tax-exempt status of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/magazine/story/2014/05/religious-right-real-origins-107133/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bob Jones University}}}\uc2 , a Christian college that defiantly refused to admit Black students on \u8220\'20\'1Creligious liberty\u8221\'20\'1D grounds. The Dartmouth University religious history professor Randall Balmer argued this was an important moment in activating white evangelical protestant voters, or the \u8220\'20\'1Cmoral majority\u8221\'20\'1D, who would join many Catholic voters in being receptive to anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  political messaging.\par \par \uc2 Evangelical opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cwas a kind of historical accident\u8221\'20\'1D, said Balmer, who details this history in his recent book Bad Faith: Race and the Rise of the Religious Right. \u8220\'20\'1CIt allowed them to deflect attention away from the real narrative and to advocate on behalf of the fetus.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 1982\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania passed among the most stringent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in the nation with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thirteen.org/wnet/supremecourt/rights/print/landmark_casey.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Control Act}}}\uc2 , which required minors to gain parental consent to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , imposed a 24-hour waiting period on women seeking abortions and required married women to notify their husbands if they were seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Although it was not the first state or city to enact such impediments, it became the most significant, because its law survived a court challenge. The early 1980s also saw the rise of the religious right with the election of President Ronald Reagan, who voiced support for a constitutional amendment to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 1992\par \par \uc2 Litigation against Pennsylvania\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Control Act reached the supreme court in a case called {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thirteen.org/wnet/supremecourt/rights/landmark_casey.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Planned Parenthood v Casey}}}\uc2 . The court upheld the essential ruling in Roe v Wade but allowed states to pass more \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions as long as they did not pose an \u8220\'20\'1Cundue burden\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The decision ushered in a new era of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, which would come to be known as Trap laws, referring to targeted regulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers. States passed hundreds of expensive and burdensome restrictions, often in an effort to test the constitutional limits of the court\u8217\'20\'19s new ruling.\par \par \uc2 The same year, President Bill Clinton said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/ideas/archive/2019/12/the-brilliance-of-safe-legal-and-rare/603151/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Csafe, legal and rare\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 , a phrase later seen as a symbol of the Democratic party\u8217\'20\'19s acceptance of the idea that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was shameful.\par \par \uc2 1993\par \par \uc2 Even as Casey ushered in a new era of incremental \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access dwindled, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  zealots saw the case as a loss. Frustrated, some turned to violence and intimidation, organizing blockades of clinics. Their tactics culminated in the assassination of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/1993/03/11/us/doctor-is-slain-during-protest-over-abortions.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dr David Gunn}}}\uc2 , who was shot dead outside an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in Pensacola, Florida. Eleven people have been murdered as a result of attacks on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers since 1977.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  forces got very extreme in the 1980s and 90s,\u8221\'20\'1D said Laura Briggs, author of How All Politics Became Reproductive Politics: from Welfare Reform to Foreclosure to Trump. \u8220\'20\'1CFirst they started bombing clinics and murdering providers, and formed the nucleus of the alt-right that we\u8217\'20\'19re now living with as white nationalists.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 1994\par \par \uc2 Two Planned Parenthood receptionists, Shannon Lowney, 25, and Leanne Nichols, 38, were killed and five more people injured at separate shootings by the same perpetrator in suburban Boston. The same year, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/1994/07/30/us/death-doctor-overview-abortion-doctor-bodyguard-slainin-florida-protester.html?pagewanted=all"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dr John Bayard Britton}}}\uc2 , 69, and James Barrett, 74, a clinic security volunteer, were shot and killed by an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremist outside another Pensacola clinic.\par \par \uc2 1998\par \par \uc2 A Canadian man shot Dr Barnett Slepian \u8211\'20\'13 a Buffalo, New York, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider \u8211\'20\'13 through his kitchen window with a high-powered rifle. The perpetrator remained free for five years before he was apprehended.\par \par \uc2 The same year, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2015/11/29/us/30abortion-clinic-violence.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a nail bomb}}}\uc2  exploded outside a Birmingham, Alabama, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, killing an off-duty police officer, Robert Sanderson. The man responsible, Eric Rudolph, eventually pleaded guilty to the 1996 bombing of the Atlanta Olympics, as well as bombings at two more \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and a gay bar.\par \par \uc2 2000\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FDA{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/gpr/2013/03/medication-abortion-restrictions-burden-women-and-providers-and-threaten-us-trend-toward"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 approved the drug mifepristone}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the first pill available to end a pregnancy early in gestation. In the coming decades, the drug proved extremely safe and effective, and was used by millions. By 2017, mifepristone accounted for 39% of all abortions.\par \par \uc2 However, it also became the target of attacks and disinformation. Some states required a doctor to be in the room when dispensing the medication, effectively {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/state-policy/explore/medication-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 banning telemedicine abortions}}}\uc2 . In others, anti-science disinformation about the ability to \u8220\'20\'1Creverse\u8221\'20\'1D a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2019/jul/25/revealed-doctor-reverse-abortion-trump-administration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 allowed to flourish}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 2007\par \par \uc2 The supreme court upheld a federal ban on dilation and extraction, a procedure opponents call \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2006/02/21/5168163/partial-birth-abortion-separating-fact-from-spin"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 partial birth \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. In a 5-4 decision, the court ruled a ban on the procedure was constitutional, even though it was designed to lead to fewer complications for pregnant people. The decision weakened Roe, which held that a pregnant person\u8217\'20\'19s health should be paramount.\par \par \uc2 2009\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2009/06/01/us/01tiller.html?pagewanted=all"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dr George Tiller}}}\uc2  was shot and killed in the entrance of his church in Wichita, Kansas, by an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremist. Tiller was one of the few \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in the country willing to perform the procedure late in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 2013\par \par \uc2 Texas passed a law that required \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to have \u8220\'20\'1Cadmitting privileges\u8221\'20\'1D at local hospitals. The requirement proved impossible for many doctors to meet, shut down dozens of clinics in Texas, and was then replicated by other states. The midwest and south became the epicenters of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation.\par \par \uc2 The same year, North Dakota passed the nation\u8217\'20\'19s first \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/sections/thetwo-way/2016/01/25/464311731/supreme-court-rejects-north-dakota-s-bid-to-save-strict-abortion-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 heartbeat bill}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D, a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after roughly six weeks. That is before most people know they are pregnant and before the vast majority of abortions take place.\par \par \uc2 At the time it was passed, mainstream anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists believed it was too extreme and would only serve to bolster Roe.\par \par \uc2 2015\par \par \uc2 A man opened fire at a Colorado Springs Planned Parenthood, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2015/11/28/us/colorado-planned-parenthood-shooting.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 killing three people and wounding nine more}}}\uc2 . His victims were later identified as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.denverpost.com/2015/12/04/jennifer-markovsky-planned-parenthood-shooting-victim-mourned/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jennifer Markovsky}}}\uc2 , 35; {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/news/us-news/planned-parenthood-victim-kearre-stewart-tried-save-others-n471126"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ke\u8217\'20\'19Arre Stewart}}}\uc2 , 29; and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://obits.gazette.com/us/obituaries/gazette/name/garrett-swasey-obituary?pid=176737046"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Garrett Swasey}}}\uc2 , 44.\par \par \uc2 The same year, the supreme court struck down Texas\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cadmitting privileges\u8221\'20\'1D law in a 5-3 decision, ruling the burdens placed on people were unconstitutional. Nevertheless, the damage was done \u8211\'20\'13 most of the clinics that closed when Texas enacted the law remain shuttered.\par \par \uc2 2016\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was elected president. Running as a Republican on the campaign trail, he said people who had abortions should be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2016/mar/30/donald-trump-women-abortions-punishment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 punished}}}\uc2  and promised to appoint supreme court justices to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2019/apr/25/the-anti-abortion-crusader-hopes-her-heartbeat-law-will-test-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cautomatically\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  overturn Roe. He was carried to victory with strong support from the religious right. Trump confirmed three justices to the court during his term in office \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2017/apr/07/senate-confirms-neil-gorsuch-supreme-court-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Neil Gorsuch}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2018/oct/06/brett-kavanaugh-confirmed-us-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/amy-coney-barrett"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 giving conservatives a 6-3 super-majority.\par \par \uc2 2019\par \par \uc2 Several more states passed so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cheartbeat bills\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 including Ohio and Georgia \u8211\'20\'13 even though the laws were blatantly unconstitutional and were blocked by courts. The author of the bills is an anti-LGBTQ+ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2019/apr/25/the-anti-abortion-crusader-hopes-her-heartbeat-law-will-test-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 conspiracy theorist}}}\uc2  whom anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists once considered too extreme, but who found new purchase in the Trump era.\par \par \uc2 Emboldened by Trump, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement sees more grassroots efforts to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  entirely. New York and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-health-us-supreme-court-illinois-f1a03ad9c1ba5dbce1293796aa72dcf8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Illinois}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nyclu.org/en/campaigns/what-you-need-know-about-reproductive-health-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 expand \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights}}}\uc2  in response to near constant attacks in other states and from the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 2020\par \par \uc2 The Covid-19 pandemic hit, and much of normal life came to a standstill. Hospitals and clinics across the country suspended normal operations. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/coronavirus-covid-19/issue-brief/state-action-to-limit-abortion-access-during-the-covid-19-pandemic/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ten states}}}\uc2  attempted to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a \u8220\'20\'1Cnonessential\u8221\'20\'1D service. Courts intervened and many restrictions were lifted, though many measures had already sown confusion.\par \par \uc2 The same year, Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , hailed by some as the court\u8217\'20\'19s greatest champion of reproductive health and freedom, died at age 87. The Trump administration quickly confirmed her successor \u8211\'20\'13 Barrett.\par \par \uc2 2021\par \par \uc2 By May, the supreme court agreed to consider Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/may/18/us-supreme-court-abortion-test-case-mississippi-precedent"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 alarming activists}}}\uc2  who worry the case could be used to significantly weaken the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The case focuses on whether Mississippi can outlaw most abortions at 15 weeks, a direct challenge to Roe, which provides a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to the point the fetus can live outside the womb, widely regarded as 24 weeks gestation. A full-term pregnancy is 39 weeks.\par \par \uc2 By {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2021/07/state-policy-trends-midyear-2021-already-worst-legislative-year-ever-us-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mid-year}}}\uc2 , 2021 has already become the most hostile year for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights legislation on record, with more than 90 restrictions put in place by states. In July, attorneys for Mississippi {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/courts_law/mississippi-abortion-supreme-court-roe-v-wade/2021/07/22/9b30cb8a-eb23-11eb-97a0-a09d10181e36_story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 asked the court}}}\uc2  to use the Dobbs case to overturn Roe v Wade, and called the 1973 decision one which has \u8220\'20\'1Cpoisoned our national discourse\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cplagued the law\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 By September, the supreme court had let Texas\u8217\'20\'19s six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban go into effect while it was litigated, halting the vast majority of abortions in the country\u8217\'20\'19s second-biggest state. Experts called Roe v Wade a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/sep/04/texas-abortion-ruling-reaction"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dessicated husk}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D of a ruling.\par \par \uc2 The court shocked observers again in September, when it allowed Texas to implement a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. In effect, Texas nullifies the constitutional rights granted by Roe v Wade within its borders.\par \par \uc2 If the Mississippi case leads to the fall of Roe v Wade when the court issues its decision in 2022, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions are left to the states, more than half would be likely to move to again make the procedure illegal.\par \par \uc2 Dr Wendy Chavkin, a special lecturer in population and family health and clinical obstetrics and gynecology at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  who helped found Global Doctors for Choice, said such a decisionwould set the US back decades.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf this thing goes wrong, we\u8217\'20\'19re bucking the historic tide\u8221\'20\'1D of liberalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws globally, said Chavkin. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19ll be like some of those decisions that the US is \u8211\'20\'13 embarrassed is an inadequate word \u8211\'20\'13 but profoundly embarrassed by for years and we\u8217\'20\'19ll have a lot of trouble ameliorating the harms that will result.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211201ehc1000ul\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\ce